0x50014868 B  REGISTER OR1: TIM17 option register 1
0x50014868 C   FIELD 00w02 RTCPREEN (rw): RTCPRE divider enable
0x50014BDC B  REGISTER DCR (=TIM16.DCR): TIM17 DMA control register
0x50014BE0 B  REGISTER DMAR (=TIM16.DMAR): TIM16/TIM17 DMA address for full transfer
0x50014C00 A PERIPHERAL SEC_SPI4
0x50014C00 B  REGISTER CR1: SPI/I2S control register 1
0x50014C00 C   FIELD 00w01 SPE (rw): serial peripheral enable This bit is set by and cleared by software. When SPE=1, SPI data transfer is enabled, SPI_CFG1 and SPI_CFG2 configuration registers, CRCPOLY, UDRDR, IOLOCK bit in the SPI_CR1 register are write protected. They can be changed only when SPE=0. When SPE=0 any SPI operation is stopped and disabled, all the pending requests of the events with enabled interrupt are blocked except the MODF interrupt request (but their pending still propagates the request of the spi_plck clock), the SS output is deactivated at master, the RDY signal keeps not ready status at slave, the internal state machine is reseted, all the FIFOs content is flushed, CRC calculation initialized, receive data register is read zero. SPE is cleared and cannot be set when MODF error flag is active.
0x50014C00 C   FIELD 08w01 MASRX (rw): master automatic suspension in Receive mode This bit is set and cleared by software to control continuous SPI transfer in master receiver mode and automatic management in order to avoid overrun condition. When SPI communication is suspended by hardware automatically, it could happen that few bits of next frame are already clocked out due to internal synchronization delay. This is why, the automatic suspension is not quite reliable when size of data drops below 8 bits. In this case, a safe suspension can be achieved by combination with delay inserted between data frames applied when MIDI parameter keeps a non zero value; sum of data size and the interleaved SPI cycles should always produce interval at length of 8 SPI clock periods at minimum. After software clearing of the SUSP bit, the communication resumes and continues by subsequent bits transaction without any next constraint. Prior the SUSP bit is cleared, the user must release the RxFIFO space as much as possible by reading out all the data packets available at RxFIFO based on the RXP flag indication to prevent any subsequent suspension.
0x50014C00 C   FIELD 09w01 CSTART (rw): master transfer start This bit can be set by software if SPI is enabled only to start an SPI or I2S/PCM communication. In SPI mode, it is cleared by hardware when end of transfer (EOT) flag is set or when a transaction suspend request is accepted. In I2S/PCM mode, it is also cleared by hardware as described in the . In SPI mode, the bit is taken into account at master mode only. If transmission is enabled, communication starts or continues only if any data is available in the transmission FIFO.
0x50014C00 C   FIELD 10w01 CSUSP (wo): master SUSPend request This bit reads as zero. In Master mode, when this bit is set by software, the CSTART bit is reset at the end of the current frame and communication is suspended. The user has to check SUSP flag to check end of the frame transaction. The Master mode communication must be suspended (using this bit or keeping TXDR empty) before going to Low-power mode. Can be used in SPI or I2S mode. After software suspension, SUSP flag has to be cleared and SPI disabled and re-enabled before the next transaction starts.
0x50014C00 C   FIELD 11w01 HDDIR (rw): Rx/Tx direction at Half-duplex mode In Half-Duplex configuration the HDDIR bit establishes the Rx/Tx direction of the data transfer. This bit is ignored in Full-Duplex or any Simplex configuration.
0x50014C00 C   FIELD 12w01 SSI (rw): internal SS signal input level This bit has an effect only when the SSM bit is set. The value of this bit is forced onto the peripheral SS input internally and the I/O value of the SS pin is ignored.
0x50014C00 C   FIELD 13w01 CRC33_17 (rw): 32-bit CRC polynomial configuration
0x50014C00 C   FIELD 14w01 RCRCINI (rw): CRC calculation initialization pattern control for receiver
0x50014C00 C   FIELD 15w01 TCRCINI (rw): CRC calculation initialization pattern control for transmitter
0x50014C00 C   FIELD 16w01 IOLOCK (rw): locking the AF configuration of associated IOs This bit is set by software and cleared by hardware whenever the SPE bit is changed from 1 to 0. When this bit is set, SPI_CFG2 register content cannot be modified. This bit can be set when SPI is disabled only else it is write protected. It is cleared and cannot be set when MODF bit is set.
0x50014C04 B  REGISTER CR2: SPI/I2S control register 2
0x50014C04 C   FIELD 00w16 TSIZE (rw): number of data at current transfer When these bits are changed by software, the SPI has to be disabled. Endless transaction is initialized when CSTART is set while zero value is stored at TSIZE. TSIZE cannot be set to 0xFFFF respective 0x3FFF value when CRC is enabled. Note: TSIZE[15:10] bits are reserved at limited feature set instances and must be kept at reset value.
0x50014C08 B  REGISTER CFG1: SPI/I2S configuration register 1
0x50014C08 C   FIELD 00w05 DSIZE (rw): number of bits in at single SPI data frame ..... Note: Maximum data size can be limited up to 16-bits at some instances. At instances with limited set of features, DSIZE2:0] bits are reserved and must be kept at reset state. DSIZE[4:3] bits then control next settings of data size: 00xxx: 8-bits 01xxx: 16-bits 10xxx: 24-bits 11xxx: 32-bits.
0x50014C08 C   FIELD 05w04 FTHLV (rw): FIFO threshold level Defines number of data frames at single data packet. Size of the packet should not exceed 1/2 of FIFO space. SPI interface is more efficient if configured packet sizes are aligned with data register access parallelism: If SPI data register is accessed as a 16-bit register and DSIZE less than or equal 8 bit, better to select FTHLV = 2, 4, 6. If SPI data register is accessed as a 32-bit register and DSIZE 8 bit, better to select FTHLV = 2, 4, 6, while if DSIZE less than or equal 8bit, better to select FTHLV = 4, 8, 12. Note: FTHLV[3:2] bits are reserved at instances with limited set of features
0x50014C08 C   FIELD 09w01 UDRCFG (rw): behavior of slave transmitter at underrun condition For more details see underrun condition.
0x50014C08 C   FIELD 14w01 RXDMAEN (rw): Rx DMA stream enable
0x50014C08 C   FIELD 15w01 TXDMAEN (rw): Tx DMA stream enable
0x50014C08 C   FIELD 16w05 CRCSIZE (rw): length of CRC frame to be transacted and compared Most significant bits are taken into account from polynomial calculation when CRC result is transacted or compared. The length of the polynomial is not affected by this setting. ..... The value must be set equal or multiply of data size (DSIZE[4:0]). Its maximum size corresponds to DSIZE maximum at the instance. Note: The most significant bit at CRCSIZE bit field is reserved at the peripheral instances where data size is limited to 16-bit.
0x50014C08 C   FIELD 22w01 CRCEN (rw): hardware CRC computation enable
0x50014C08 C   FIELD 28w03 MBR (rw): master baud rate prescaler setting Note: MBR setting is considered at slave working at TI mode, too (see mode).
0x50014C08 C   FIELD 31w01 BPASS (rw): bypass of the prescaler at master baud rate clock generator
0x50014C0C B  REGISTER CFG2: SPI/I2S configuration register 2
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 00w04 MSSI (rw): Master SS Idleness Specifies an extra delay, expressed in number of SPI clock cycle periods, inserted additionally between active edge of SS opening a session and the beginning of the first data frame of the session in Master mode when SSOE is enabled. ... Note: This feature is not supported in TI mode. To include the delay, the SPI must be disabled and re-enabled between sessions.
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 04w04 MIDI (rw): master Inter-Data Idleness Specifies minimum time delay (expressed in SPI clock cycles periods) inserted between two consecutive data frames in Master mode. ... Note: This feature is not supported in TI mode.
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 13w01 RDIOM (rw): RDY signal input/output management Note: When DSIZE at the SPI_CFG1 register is configured shorter than 8-bit, the RDIOM bit has to be kept at zero.
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 14w01 RDIOP (rw): RDY signal input/output polarity
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 15w01 IOSWP (rw): swap functionality of MISO and MOSI pins When this bit is set, the function of MISO and MOSI pins alternate functions are inverted. Original MISO pin becomes MOSI and original MOSI pin becomes MISO. Note: This bit can be also used in PCM and I2S modes to swap SDO and SDI pins.
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 17w02 COMM (rw): SPI Communication Mode
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 19w03 SP (rw): serial protocol others: reserved, must not be used
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 22w01 MASTER (rw): SPI Master
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 23w01 LSBFRST (rw): data frame format Note: This bit can be also used in PCM and I2S modes.
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 24w01 CPHA (rw): clock phase
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 25w01 CPOL (rw): clock polarity
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 26w01 SSM (rw): software management of SS signal input When master uses hardware SS output (SSM=0 and SSOE=1) the SS signal input is forced to not active state internally to prevent master mode fault error.
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 28w01 SSIOP (rw): SS input/output polarity
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 29w01 SSOE (rw): SS output enable This bit is taken into account in Master mode only
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 30w01 SSOM (rw): SS output management in Master mode This bit is taken into account in Master mode when SSOE is enabled. It allows the SS output to be configured between two consecutive data transfers.
0x50014C0C C   FIELD 31w01 AFCNTR (rw): alternate function GPIOs control This bit is taken into account when SPE=0 only When SPI has to be disabled temporary for a specific configuration reason (e.g. CRC reset, CPHA or HDDIR change) setting this bit prevents any glitches on the associated outputs configured at alternate function mode by keeping them forced at state corresponding the current SPI configuration. Note: This bit can be also used in PCM and I2S modes. Note: The bit AFCNTR must not be set to 1, when the block is in slave mode.
0x50014C10 B  REGISTER IER: SPI/I2S interrupt enable register
0x50014C10 C   FIELD 00w01 RXPIE (rw): RXP interrupt enable
0x50014C10 C   FIELD 01w01 TXPIE (rw): TXP interrupt enable TXPIE is set by software and cleared by TXTF flag set event.
0x50014C10 C   FIELD 02w01 DXPIE (rw): DXP interrupt enabled DXPIE is set by software and cleared by TXTF flag set event.
0x50014C10 C   FIELD 03w01 EOTIE (rw): EOT, SUSP and TXC interrupt enable
0x50014C10 C   FIELD 04w01 TXTFIE (rw): TXTFIE interrupt enable
0x50014C10 C   FIELD 05w01 UDRIE (rw): UDR interrupt enable
0x50014C10 C   FIELD 06w01 OVRIE (rw): OVR interrupt enable
0x50014C10 C   FIELD 07w01 CRCEIE (rw): CRC error interrupt enable
0x50014C10 C   FIELD 08w01 TIFREIE (rw): TIFRE interrupt enable
0x50014C10 C   FIELD 09w01 MODFIE (rw): mode Fault interrupt enable
0x50014C14 B  REGISTER SR: SPI/I2S status register
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 00w01 RXP (ro): Rx-Packet available In I2S mode, it must be interpreted as follow: RxFIFO level is lower than FTHLV In I2S mode, it must be interpreted as follow: RxFIFO level is higher or equal to FTHLV RXP flag is changed by hardware. It monitors number of overall data currently available at RxFIFO if SPI is enabled. It has to be checked once a data packet is completely read out from RxFIFO.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 01w01 TXP (ro): Tx-Packet space available In I2S mode, it must be interpreted as follow: there is less than FTHLV free locations in the TxFIFO In I2S mode, it must be interpreted as follow: there is FTHLV or more than FTHLV free locations in the TxFIFO TXP flag is changed by hardware. It monitors overall space currently available at TxFIFO no matter if SPI is enabled or not. It has to be checked once a complete data packet is stored at TxFIFO.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 02w01 DXP (ro): duplex packet DXP flag is set whenever both TXP and RXP flags are set regardless SPI mode.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 03w01 EOT (ro): end of transfer EOT is set by hardware as soon as a full transfer is complete, that is when SPI is re-enabled or when TSIZE number of data have been transmitted and/or received on the SPI. EOT is cleared when SPI is re-enabled or by writing 1 to EOTC bit of SPI_IFCR optionally. EOT flag triggers an interrupt if EOTIE bit is set. If DXP flag is used until TXTF flag is set and DXPIE is cleared, EOT can be used to download the last packets contained into RxFIFO in one-shot. In master, EOT event terminates the data transaction and handles SS output optionally. When CRC is applied, the EOT event is extended over the CRC frame transaction. To restart the internal state machine properly, SPI is strongly suggested to be disabled and re-enabled before next transaction starts despite its setting is not changed.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 04w01 TXTF (ro): transmission transfer filled TXTF is set by hardware as soon as all of the data packets in a transfer have been submitted for transmission by application software or DMA, that is when TSIZE number of data have been pushed into the TxFIFO. This bit is cleared by software write 1 to TXTFC bit of SPI_IFCR exclusively. TXTF flag triggers an interrupt if TXTFIE bit is set. TXTF setting clears the TXPIE and DXPIE masks so to off-load application software from calculating when to disable TXP and DXP interrupts.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 05w01 UDR (ro): underrun This bit is cleared when SPI is re-enabled or by writing 1 to UDRC bit of SPI_IFCR optionally. Note: In SPI mode, the UDR flag applies to Slave mode only. In I2S/PCM mode, (when available) this flag applies to Master and Slave mode
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 06w01 OVR (ro): overrun This bit is cleared when SPI is re-enabled or by writing 1 to OVRC bit of SPI_IFCR optionally.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 07w01 CRCE (ro): CRC error This bit is cleared when SPI is re-enabled or by writing 1 to CRCEC bit of SPI_IFCR optionally.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 08w01 TIFRE (ro): TI frame format error This bit is cleared by writing 1 to TIFREC bit of SPI_IFCR exclusively.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 09w01 MODF (ro): mode fault This bit is cleared by writing 1 to MODFC bit of SPI_IFCR exclusively.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 11w01 SUSP (ro): suspension status In Master mode, SUSP is set by hardware either as soon as the current frame is completed after CSUSP request is done or at master automatic suspend receive mode (MASRX bit is set at SPI_CR1 register) on RxFIFO full condition. SUSP generates an interrupt when EOTIE is set. This bit has to be cleared prior SPI is disabled and this is done by writing 1 to SUSPC bit of SPI_IFCR exclusively.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 12w01 TXC (ro): TxFIFO transmission complete The flag behavior depends on TSIZE setting. When TSIZE=0 the TXC is changed by hardware exclusively and it raises each time the TxFIFO becomes empty and there is no activity on the bus. If TSIZE 0 there is no specific reason to monitor TXC as it just copies the EOT flag value including its software clearing. The TXC generates an interrupt when EOTIE is set.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 13w02 RXPLVL (ro): RxFIFO packing level When RXWNE=0 and data size is set up to 16-bit, the value gives number of remaining data frames persisting at RxFIFO. Note: (*): Optional value when data size is set up to 8-bit only. When data size is greater than 16-bit, these bits are always read as 00. In that consequence, the single data frame received at the FIFO cannot be detected neither by RWNE nor by RXPLVL bits if data size is set from 17 to 24 bits. The user then must apply other methods like TSIZE 0 or FTHLV=0.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 15w01 RXWNE (ro): RxFIFO word not empty Note: This bit value does not depend on DSIZE setting and keeps together with RXPLVL[1:0] information about RxFIFO occupancy by residual data.
0x50014C14 C   FIELD 16w16 CTSIZE (ro): number of data frames remaining in current TSIZE session The value is not quite reliable when traffic is ongoing on bus or during autonomous operation in low-power mode. Note: CTSIZE[15:0] bits are not available in instances with limited set of features.
0x50014C18 B  REGISTER IFCR: SPI/I2S interrupt/status flags clear register
0x50014C18 C   FIELD 03w01 EOTC (wo): end of transfer flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears EOT flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50014C18 C   FIELD 04w01 TXTFC (wo): transmission transfer filled flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears TXTF flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50014C18 C   FIELD 05w01 UDRC (wo): underrun flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears UDR flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50014C18 C   FIELD 06w01 OVRC (wo): overrun flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears OVR flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50014C18 C   FIELD 07w01 CRCEC (wo): CRC error flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears CRCE flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50014C18 C   FIELD 08w01 TIFREC (wo): TI frame format error flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears TIFRE flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50014C18 C   FIELD 09w01 MODFC (wo): mode fault flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears MODF flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50014C18 C   FIELD 11w01 SUSPC (wo): SUSPend flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears SUSP flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50014C20 B  REGISTER TXDR16 (wo): Direct 16-bit access to transmit data register
0x50014C20 B  REGISTER TXDR8 (wo): Direct 8-bit access to transmit data register
0x50014C20 B  REGISTER TXDR: SPI/I2S transmit data register
0x50014C20 C   FIELD 00w08 TXDR: Transmit data register
0x50014C20 C   FIELD 00w16 TXDR: Transmit data register
0x50014C20 C   FIELD 00w32 TXDR (wo): transmit data register The register serves as an interface with TxFIFO. A write to it accesses TxFIFO. Note: In SPI mode, data is always right-aligned. Alignment of data at I2S mode depends on DATLEN and DATFMT setting. Unused bits are ignored when writing to the register, and read as zero when the register is read. Note: DR can be accessed byte-wise (8-bit access): in this case only one data-byte is written by single access. halfword-wise (16 bit access) in this case 2 data-bytes or 1 halfword-data can be written by single access. word-wise (32 bit access). In this case 4 data-bytes or 2 halfword-data or word-data can be written by single access. Write access of this register less than the configured data size is forbidden.
0x50014C30 B  REGISTER RXDR16 (ro): Direct 16-bit access to receive data register
0x50014C30 B  REGISTER RXDR8 (ro): Direct 8-bit access to receive data register
0x50014C30 B  REGISTER RXDR: SPI/I2S receive data register
0x50014C30 C   FIELD 00w08 RXDR: Receive data register
0x50014C30 C   FIELD 00w16 RXDR: Receive data register
0x50014C30 C   FIELD 00w32 RXDR (ro): receive data register The register serves as an interface with RxFIFO. When it is read, RxFIFO is accessed. Note: In SPI mode, data is always right-aligned. Alignment of data at I2S mode depends on DATLEN and DATFMT setting. Unused bits are read as zero when the register is read. Writing to the register is ignored. Note: DR can be accessed byte-wise (8-bit access): in this case only one data-byte is read by single access halfword-wise (16 bit access) in this case 2 data-bytes or 1 halfword-data can be read by single access word-wise (32 bit access). In this case 4 data-bytes or 2 halfword-data or word-data can be read by single access. Read access of this register less than the configured data size is forbidden.
0x50014C40 B  REGISTER CRCPOLY: SPI/I2S polynomial register
0x50014C40 C   FIELD 00w32 CRCPOLY (rw): CRC polynomial register This register contains the polynomial for the CRC calculation. The default 9-bit polynomial setting 0x107 corresponds to default 8-bit setting of DSIZE. It is compatible with setting 0x07 used at some other ST products with fixed length of the polynomial string where the most significant bit of the string is always kept hidden. Length of the polynomial is given by the most significant bit of the value stored at this register. It has to be set greater than DSIZE. CRC33_17 bit has to be set additionally with CRCPOLY register when DSIZE is configured to maximum 32-bit or 16-bit size and CRC is enabled (to keep polynomial length grater than data size). Note: CRCPOLY[31:16] bits are reserved at instances with data size limited to 16-bit. There is no constrain when 32-bit access is applied at these addresses. Reserved bits 31-16 are always read zero while any write to them is ignored.
0x50014C44 B  REGISTER TXCRC: SPI/I2S transmitter CRC register
0x50014C44 C   FIELD 00w32 TXCRC (ro): CRC register for transmitter When CRC calculation is enabled, the TXCRC[31:0] bits contain the computed CRC value of the subsequently transmitted bytes. CRC calculation is initialized when the CRCEN bit of SPI_CR1 is written to 1 or when a data block is transacted completely. The CRC is calculated serially using the polynomial programmed in the SPI_CRCPOLY register. The number of bits considered at calculation depends on SPI_CRCPOLY register and CRCSIZE bits settings at SPI_CFG1 register. Note: a read to this register when the communication is ongoing could return an incorrect value. Note: not used for the I2S mode. Note: TXCRC[31-16] bits are reserved at instances with data size limited to 16-bit. There is no constrain when 32-bit access is applied at these addresses. Reserved bits 31-16 are always read zero while any write to them is ignored. Note: The configuration of CRCSIZE bit field is not taken into account when the content of this register is read by software. No masking is applied for unused bits at this case.
0x50014C48 B  REGISTER RXCRC: SPI/I2S receiver CRC register
0x50014C48 C   FIELD 00w32 RXCRC (ro): CRC register for receiver When CRC calculation is enabled, the RXCRC[31:0] bits contain the computed CRC value of the subsequently received bytes. CRC calculation is initialized when the CRCEN bit of SPI_CR1 is written to 1 or when a data block is transacted completely. The CRC is calculated serially using the polynomial programmed in the SPI_CRCPOLY register. The number of bits considered at calculation depends on SPI_CRCPOLY register and CRCSIZE bits settings at SPI_CFG1 register. Note: a read to this register when the communication is ongoing could return an incorrect value. Not used for the I2S mode. RXCRC[31-16] bits are reserved at the peripheral instances with data size limited to 16-bit. There is no constrain when 32-bit access is applied at these addresses. Reserved bits 31-16 are always read zero while any write to them is ignored. Note: The configuration of CRCSIZE bit field is not taken into account when the content of this register is read by software. No masking is applied for unused bits at this case.
0x50014C4C B  REGISTER UDRDR: SPI/I2S underrun data register
0x50014C4C C   FIELD 00w32 UDRDR (rw): data at slave underrun condition The register is taken into account in Slave mode and at underrun condition only. The number of bits considered depends on DSIZE bit settings of the SPI_CFG1 register. Underrun condition handling depends on setting UDRCFG bit at SPI_CFG1 register. Note: UDRDR[31-16] bits are reserved at the peripheral instances with data size limited to 16-bit. There is no constraint when 32-bit access is applied at these addresses. Reserved bits 31-16 are always read zero while any write to them is ignored.
0x50014C50 B  REGISTER I2SCFGR: SPI/I2S configuration register
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 00w01 I2SMOD (rw): I2S mode selection
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 01w03 I2SCFG (rw): I2S configuration mode others, not used
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 04w02 I2SSTD (rw): I2S standard selection For more details on I2S standards, refer to
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 07w01 PCMSYNC (rw): PCM frame synchronization
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 08w02 DATLEN (rw): data length to be transferred
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 10w01 CHLEN (rw): channel length (number of bits per audio channel)
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 11w01 CKPOL (rw): serial audio clock polarity
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 12w01 FIXCH (rw): fixed channel length in slave
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 13w01 WSINV (rw): word select inversion This bit is used to invert the default polarity of WS signal. WS is LOW. In PCM mode the start of frame is indicated by a rising edge. WS is HIGH. In PCM mode the start of frame is indicated by a falling edge.
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 14w01 DATFMT (rw): data format
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 16w08 I2SDIV (rw): I2S linear prescaler I2SDIV can take any values except the value 1, when ODD is also equal to 1. Refer to for details
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 24w01 ODD (rw): odd factor for the prescaler Refer to for details
0x50014C50 C   FIELD 25w01 MCKOE (rw): master clock output enable
0x50015000 A PERIPHERAL SEC_SPI6
0x50015000 B  REGISTER CR1: SPI/I2S control register 1
0x50015000 C   FIELD 00w01 SPE (rw): serial peripheral enable This bit is set by and cleared by software. When SPE=1, SPI data transfer is enabled, SPI_CFG1 and SPI_CFG2 configuration registers, CRCPOLY, UDRDR, IOLOCK bit in the SPI_CR1 register are write protected. They can be changed only when SPE=0. When SPE=0 any SPI operation is stopped and disabled, all the pending requests of the events with enabled interrupt are blocked except the MODF interrupt request (but their pending still propagates the request of the spi_plck clock), the SS output is deactivated at master, the RDY signal keeps not ready status at slave, the internal state machine is reseted, all the FIFOs content is flushed, CRC calculation initialized, receive data register is read zero. SPE is cleared and cannot be set when MODF error flag is active.
0x50015000 C   FIELD 08w01 MASRX (rw): master automatic suspension in Receive mode This bit is set and cleared by software to control continuous SPI transfer in master receiver mode and automatic management in order to avoid overrun condition. When SPI communication is suspended by hardware automatically, it could happen that few bits of next frame are already clocked out due to internal synchronization delay. This is why, the automatic suspension is not quite reliable when size of data drops below 8 bits. In this case, a safe suspension can be achieved by combination with delay inserted between data frames applied when MIDI parameter keeps a non zero value; sum of data size and the interleaved SPI cycles should always produce interval at length of 8 SPI clock periods at minimum. After software clearing of the SUSP bit, the communication resumes and continues by subsequent bits transaction without any next constraint. Prior the SUSP bit is cleared, the user must release the RxFIFO space as much as possible by reading out all the data packets available at RxFIFO based on the RXP flag indication to prevent any subsequent suspension.
0x50015000 C   FIELD 09w01 CSTART (rw): master transfer start This bit can be set by software if SPI is enabled only to start an SPI or I2S/PCM communication. In SPI mode, it is cleared by hardware when end of transfer (EOT) flag is set or when a transaction suspend request is accepted. In I2S/PCM mode, it is also cleared by hardware as described in the . In SPI mode, the bit is taken into account at master mode only. If transmission is enabled, communication starts or continues only if any data is available in the transmission FIFO.
0x50015000 C   FIELD 10w01 CSUSP (wo): master SUSPend request This bit reads as zero. In Master mode, when this bit is set by software, the CSTART bit is reset at the end of the current frame and communication is suspended. The user has to check SUSP flag to check end of the frame transaction. The Master mode communication must be suspended (using this bit or keeping TXDR empty) before going to Low-power mode. Can be used in SPI or I2S mode. After software suspension, SUSP flag has to be cleared and SPI disabled and re-enabled before the next transaction starts.
0x50015000 C   FIELD 11w01 HDDIR (rw): Rx/Tx direction at Half-duplex mode In Half-Duplex configuration the HDDIR bit establishes the Rx/Tx direction of the data transfer. This bit is ignored in Full-Duplex or any Simplex configuration.
0x50015000 C   FIELD 12w01 SSI (rw): internal SS signal input level This bit has an effect only when the SSM bit is set. The value of this bit is forced onto the peripheral SS input internally and the I/O value of the SS pin is ignored.
0x50015000 C   FIELD 13w01 CRC33_17 (rw): 32-bit CRC polynomial configuration
0x50015000 C   FIELD 14w01 RCRCINI (rw): CRC calculation initialization pattern control for receiver
0x50015000 C   FIELD 15w01 TCRCINI (rw): CRC calculation initialization pattern control for transmitter
0x50015000 C   FIELD 16w01 IOLOCK (rw): locking the AF configuration of associated IOs This bit is set by software and cleared by hardware whenever the SPE bit is changed from 1 to 0. When this bit is set, SPI_CFG2 register content cannot be modified. This bit can be set when SPI is disabled only else it is write protected. It is cleared and cannot be set when MODF bit is set.
0x50015004 B  REGISTER CR2: SPI/I2S control register 2
0x50015004 C   FIELD 00w16 TSIZE (rw): number of data at current transfer When these bits are changed by software, the SPI has to be disabled. Endless transaction is initialized when CSTART is set while zero value is stored at TSIZE. TSIZE cannot be set to 0xFFFF respective 0x3FFF value when CRC is enabled. Note: TSIZE[15:10] bits are reserved at limited feature set instances and must be kept at reset value.
0x50015008 B  REGISTER CFG1: SPI/I2S configuration register 1
0x50015008 C   FIELD 00w05 DSIZE (rw): number of bits in at single SPI data frame ..... Note: Maximum data size can be limited up to 16-bits at some instances. At instances with limited set of features, DSIZE2:0] bits are reserved and must be kept at reset state. DSIZE[4:3] bits then control next settings of data size: 00xxx: 8-bits 01xxx: 16-bits 10xxx: 24-bits 11xxx: 32-bits.
0x50015008 C   FIELD 05w04 FTHLV (rw): FIFO threshold level Defines number of data frames at single data packet. Size of the packet should not exceed 1/2 of FIFO space. SPI interface is more efficient if configured packet sizes are aligned with data register access parallelism: If SPI data register is accessed as a 16-bit register and DSIZE less than or equal 8 bit, better to select FTHLV = 2, 4, 6. If SPI data register is accessed as a 32-bit register and DSIZE 8 bit, better to select FTHLV = 2, 4, 6, while if DSIZE less than or equal 8bit, better to select FTHLV = 4, 8, 12. Note: FTHLV[3:2] bits are reserved at instances with limited set of features
0x50015008 C   FIELD 09w01 UDRCFG (rw): behavior of slave transmitter at underrun condition For more details see underrun condition.
0x50015008 C   FIELD 14w01 RXDMAEN (rw): Rx DMA stream enable
0x50015008 C   FIELD 15w01 TXDMAEN (rw): Tx DMA stream enable
0x50015008 C   FIELD 16w05 CRCSIZE (rw): length of CRC frame to be transacted and compared Most significant bits are taken into account from polynomial calculation when CRC result is transacted or compared. The length of the polynomial is not affected by this setting. ..... The value must be set equal or multiply of data size (DSIZE[4:0]). Its maximum size corresponds to DSIZE maximum at the instance. Note: The most significant bit at CRCSIZE bit field is reserved at the peripheral instances where data size is limited to 16-bit.
0x50015008 C   FIELD 22w01 CRCEN (rw): hardware CRC computation enable
0x50015008 C   FIELD 28w03 MBR (rw): master baud rate prescaler setting Note: MBR setting is considered at slave working at TI mode, too (see mode).
0x50015008 C   FIELD 31w01 BPASS (rw): bypass of the prescaler at master baud rate clock generator
0x5001500C B  REGISTER CFG2: SPI/I2S configuration register 2
0x5001500C C   FIELD 00w04 MSSI (rw): Master SS Idleness Specifies an extra delay, expressed in number of SPI clock cycle periods, inserted additionally between active edge of SS opening a session and the beginning of the first data frame of the session in Master mode when SSOE is enabled. ... Note: This feature is not supported in TI mode. To include the delay, the SPI must be disabled and re-enabled between sessions.
0x5001500C C   FIELD 04w04 MIDI (rw): master Inter-Data Idleness Specifies minimum time delay (expressed in SPI clock cycles periods) inserted between two consecutive data frames in Master mode. ... Note: This feature is not supported in TI mode.
0x5001500C C   FIELD 13w01 RDIOM (rw): RDY signal input/output management Note: When DSIZE at the SPI_CFG1 register is configured shorter than 8-bit, the RDIOM bit has to be kept at zero.
0x5001500C C   FIELD 14w01 RDIOP (rw): RDY signal input/output polarity
0x5001500C C   FIELD 15w01 IOSWP (rw): swap functionality of MISO and MOSI pins When this bit is set, the function of MISO and MOSI pins alternate functions are inverted. Original MISO pin becomes MOSI and original MOSI pin becomes MISO. Note: This bit can be also used in PCM and I2S modes to swap SDO and SDI pins.
0x5001500C C   FIELD 17w02 COMM (rw): SPI Communication Mode
0x5001500C C   FIELD 19w03 SP (rw): serial protocol others: reserved, must not be used
0x5001500C C   FIELD 22w01 MASTER (rw): SPI Master
0x5001500C C   FIELD 23w01 LSBFRST (rw): data frame format Note: This bit can be also used in PCM and I2S modes.
0x5001500C C   FIELD 24w01 CPHA (rw): clock phase
0x5001500C C   FIELD 25w01 CPOL (rw): clock polarity
0x5001500C C   FIELD 26w01 SSM (rw): software management of SS signal input When master uses hardware SS output (SSM=0 and SSOE=1) the SS signal input is forced to not active state internally to prevent master mode fault error.
0x5001500C C   FIELD 28w01 SSIOP (rw): SS input/output polarity
0x5001500C C   FIELD 29w01 SSOE (rw): SS output enable This bit is taken into account in Master mode only
0x5001500C C   FIELD 30w01 SSOM (rw): SS output management in Master mode This bit is taken into account in Master mode when SSOE is enabled. It allows the SS output to be configured between two consecutive data transfers.
0x5001500C C   FIELD 31w01 AFCNTR (rw): alternate function GPIOs control This bit is taken into account when SPE=0 only When SPI has to be disabled temporary for a specific configuration reason (e.g. CRC reset, CPHA or HDDIR change) setting this bit prevents any glitches on the associated outputs configured at alternate function mode by keeping them forced at state corresponding the current SPI configuration. Note: This bit can be also used in PCM and I2S modes. Note: The bit AFCNTR must not be set to 1, when the block is in slave mode.
0x50015010 B  REGISTER IER: SPI/I2S interrupt enable register
0x50015010 C   FIELD 00w01 RXPIE (rw): RXP interrupt enable
0x50015010 C   FIELD 01w01 TXPIE (rw): TXP interrupt enable TXPIE is set by software and cleared by TXTF flag set event.
0x50015010 C   FIELD 02w01 DXPIE (rw): DXP interrupt enabled DXPIE is set by software and cleared by TXTF flag set event.
0x50015010 C   FIELD 03w01 EOTIE (rw): EOT, SUSP and TXC interrupt enable
0x50015010 C   FIELD 04w01 TXTFIE (rw): TXTFIE interrupt enable
0x50015010 C   FIELD 05w01 UDRIE (rw): UDR interrupt enable
0x50015010 C   FIELD 06w01 OVRIE (rw): OVR interrupt enable
0x50015010 C   FIELD 07w01 CRCEIE (rw): CRC error interrupt enable
0x50015010 C   FIELD 08w01 TIFREIE (rw): TIFRE interrupt enable
0x50015010 C   FIELD 09w01 MODFIE (rw): mode Fault interrupt enable
0x50015014 B  REGISTER SR: SPI/I2S status register
0x50015014 C   FIELD 00w01 RXP (ro): Rx-Packet available In I2S mode, it must be interpreted as follow: RxFIFO level is lower than FTHLV In I2S mode, it must be interpreted as follow: RxFIFO level is higher or equal to FTHLV RXP flag is changed by hardware. It monitors number of overall data currently available at RxFIFO if SPI is enabled. It has to be checked once a data packet is completely read out from RxFIFO.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 01w01 TXP (ro): Tx-Packet space available In I2S mode, it must be interpreted as follow: there is less than FTHLV free locations in the TxFIFO In I2S mode, it must be interpreted as follow: there is FTHLV or more than FTHLV free locations in the TxFIFO TXP flag is changed by hardware. It monitors overall space currently available at TxFIFO no matter if SPI is enabled or not. It has to be checked once a complete data packet is stored at TxFIFO.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 02w01 DXP (ro): duplex packet DXP flag is set whenever both TXP and RXP flags are set regardless SPI mode.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 03w01 EOT (ro): end of transfer EOT is set by hardware as soon as a full transfer is complete, that is when SPI is re-enabled or when TSIZE number of data have been transmitted and/or received on the SPI. EOT is cleared when SPI is re-enabled or by writing 1 to EOTC bit of SPI_IFCR optionally. EOT flag triggers an interrupt if EOTIE bit is set. If DXP flag is used until TXTF flag is set and DXPIE is cleared, EOT can be used to download the last packets contained into RxFIFO in one-shot. In master, EOT event terminates the data transaction and handles SS output optionally. When CRC is applied, the EOT event is extended over the CRC frame transaction. To restart the internal state machine properly, SPI is strongly suggested to be disabled and re-enabled before next transaction starts despite its setting is not changed.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 04w01 TXTF (ro): transmission transfer filled TXTF is set by hardware as soon as all of the data packets in a transfer have been submitted for transmission by application software or DMA, that is when TSIZE number of data have been pushed into the TxFIFO. This bit is cleared by software write 1 to TXTFC bit of SPI_IFCR exclusively. TXTF flag triggers an interrupt if TXTFIE bit is set. TXTF setting clears the TXPIE and DXPIE masks so to off-load application software from calculating when to disable TXP and DXP interrupts.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 05w01 UDR (ro): underrun This bit is cleared when SPI is re-enabled or by writing 1 to UDRC bit of SPI_IFCR optionally. Note: In SPI mode, the UDR flag applies to Slave mode only. In I2S/PCM mode, (when available) this flag applies to Master and Slave mode
0x50015014 C   FIELD 06w01 OVR (ro): overrun This bit is cleared when SPI is re-enabled or by writing 1 to OVRC bit of SPI_IFCR optionally.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 07w01 CRCE (ro): CRC error This bit is cleared when SPI is re-enabled or by writing 1 to CRCEC bit of SPI_IFCR optionally.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 08w01 TIFRE (ro): TI frame format error This bit is cleared by writing 1 to TIFREC bit of SPI_IFCR exclusively.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 09w01 MODF (ro): mode fault This bit is cleared by writing 1 to MODFC bit of SPI_IFCR exclusively.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 11w01 SUSP (ro): suspension status In Master mode, SUSP is set by hardware either as soon as the current frame is completed after CSUSP request is done or at master automatic suspend receive mode (MASRX bit is set at SPI_CR1 register) on RxFIFO full condition. SUSP generates an interrupt when EOTIE is set. This bit has to be cleared prior SPI is disabled and this is done by writing 1 to SUSPC bit of SPI_IFCR exclusively.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 12w01 TXC (ro): TxFIFO transmission complete The flag behavior depends on TSIZE setting. When TSIZE=0 the TXC is changed by hardware exclusively and it raises each time the TxFIFO becomes empty and there is no activity on the bus. If TSIZE 0 there is no specific reason to monitor TXC as it just copies the EOT flag value including its software clearing. The TXC generates an interrupt when EOTIE is set.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 13w02 RXPLVL (ro): RxFIFO packing level When RXWNE=0 and data size is set up to 16-bit, the value gives number of remaining data frames persisting at RxFIFO. Note: (*): Optional value when data size is set up to 8-bit only. When data size is greater than 16-bit, these bits are always read as 00. In that consequence, the single data frame received at the FIFO cannot be detected neither by RWNE nor by RXPLVL bits if data size is set from 17 to 24 bits. The user then must apply other methods like TSIZE 0 or FTHLV=0.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 15w01 RXWNE (ro): RxFIFO word not empty Note: This bit value does not depend on DSIZE setting and keeps together with RXPLVL[1:0] information about RxFIFO occupancy by residual data.
0x50015014 C   FIELD 16w16 CTSIZE (ro): number of data frames remaining in current TSIZE session The value is not quite reliable when traffic is ongoing on bus or during autonomous operation in low-power mode. Note: CTSIZE[15:0] bits are not available in instances with limited set of features.
0x50015018 B  REGISTER IFCR: SPI/I2S interrupt/status flags clear register
0x50015018 C   FIELD 03w01 EOTC (wo): end of transfer flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears EOT flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50015018 C   FIELD 04w01 TXTFC (wo): transmission transfer filled flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears TXTF flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50015018 C   FIELD 05w01 UDRC (wo): underrun flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears UDR flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50015018 C   FIELD 06w01 OVRC (wo): overrun flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears OVR flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50015018 C   FIELD 07w01 CRCEC (wo): CRC error flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears CRCE flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50015018 C   FIELD 08w01 TIFREC (wo): TI frame format error flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears TIFRE flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50015018 C   FIELD 09w01 MODFC (wo): mode fault flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears MODF flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50015018 C   FIELD 11w01 SUSPC (wo): SUSPend flag clear Writing a 1 into this bit clears SUSP flag in the SPI_SR register
0x50015020 B  REGISTER TXDR16 (wo): Direct 16-bit access to transmit data register
0x50015020 B  REGISTER TXDR8 (wo): Direct 8-bit access to transmit data register
0x50015020 B  REGISTER TXDR: SPI/I2S transmit data register
0x50015020 C   FIELD 00w08 TXDR: Transmit data register
0x50015020 C   FIELD 00w16 TXDR: Transmit data register
0x50015020 C   FIELD 00w32 TXDR (wo): transmit data register The register serves as an interface with TxFIFO. A write to it accesses TxFIFO. Note: In SPI mode, data is always right-aligned. Alignment of data at I2S mode depends on DATLEN and DATFMT setting. Unused bits are ignored when writing to the register, and read as zero when the register is read. Note: DR can be accessed byte-wise (8-bit access): in this case only one data-byte is written by single access. halfword-wise (16 bit access) in this case 2 data-bytes or 1 halfword-data can be written by single access. word-wise (32 bit access). In this case 4 data-bytes or 2 halfword-data or word-data can be written by single access. Write access of this register less than the configured data size is forbidden.
0x50015030 B  REGISTER RXDR16 (ro): Direct 16-bit access to receive data register
0x50015030 B  REGISTER RXDR8 (ro): Direct 8-bit access to receive data register
0x50015030 B  REGISTER RXDR: SPI/I2S receive data register
0x50015030 C   FIELD 00w08 RXDR: Receive data register
0x50015030 C   FIELD 00w16 RXDR: Receive data register
0x50015030 C   FIELD 00w32 RXDR (ro): receive data register The register serves as an interface with RxFIFO. When it is read, RxFIFO is accessed. Note: In SPI mode, data is always right-aligned. Alignment of data at I2S mode depends on DATLEN and DATFMT setting. Unused bits are read as zero when the register is read. Writing to the register is ignored. Note: DR can be accessed byte-wise (8-bit access): in this case only one data-byte is read by single access halfword-wise (16 bit access) in this case 2 data-bytes or 1 halfword-data can be read by single access word-wise (32 bit access). In this case 4 data-bytes or 2 halfword-data or word-data can be read by single access. Read access of this register less than the configured data size is forbidden.
0x50015040 B  REGISTER CRCPOLY: SPI/I2S polynomial register
0x50015040 C   FIELD 00w32 CRCPOLY (rw): CRC polynomial register This register contains the polynomial for the CRC calculation. The default 9-bit polynomial setting 0x107 corresponds to default 8-bit setting of DSIZE. It is compatible with setting 0x07 used at some other ST products with fixed length of the polynomial string where the most significant bit of the string is always kept hidden. Length of the polynomial is given by the most significant bit of the value stored at this register. It has to be set greater than DSIZE. CRC33_17 bit has to be set additionally with CRCPOLY register when DSIZE is configured to maximum 32-bit or 16-bit size and CRC is enabled (to keep polynomial length grater than data size). Note: CRCPOLY[31:16] bits are reserved at instances with data size limited to 16-bit. There is no constrain when 32-bit access is applied at these addresses. Reserved bits 31-16 are always read zero while any write to them is ignored.
0x50015044 B  REGISTER TXCRC: SPI/I2S transmitter CRC register
0x50015044 C   FIELD 00w32 TXCRC (ro): CRC register for transmitter When CRC calculation is enabled, the TXCRC[31:0] bits contain the computed CRC value of the subsequently transmitted bytes. CRC calculation is initialized when the CRCEN bit of SPI_CR1 is written to 1 or when a data block is transacted completely. The CRC is calculated serially using the polynomial programmed in the SPI_CRCPOLY register. The number of bits considered at calculation depends on SPI_CRCPOLY register and CRCSIZE bits settings at SPI_CFG1 register. Note: a read to this register when the communication is ongoing could return an incorrect value. Note: not used for the I2S mode. Note: TXCRC[31-16] bits are reserved at instances with data size limited to 16-bit. There is no constrain when 32-bit access is applied at these addresses. Reserved bits 31-16 are always read zero while any write to them is ignored. Note: The configuration of CRCSIZE bit field is not taken into account when the content of this register is read by software. No masking is applied for unused bits at this case.
0x50015048 B  REGISTER RXCRC: SPI/I2S receiver CRC register
0x50015048 C   FIELD 00w32 RXCRC (ro): CRC register for receiver When CRC calculation is enabled, the RXCRC[31:0] bits contain the computed CRC value of the subsequently received bytes. CRC calculation is initialized when the CRCEN bit of SPI_CR1 is written to 1 or when a data block is transacted completely. The CRC is calculated serially using the polynomial programmed in the SPI_CRCPOLY register. The number of bits considered at calculation depends on SPI_CRCPOLY register and CRCSIZE bits settings at SPI_CFG1 register. Note: a read to this register when the communication is ongoing could return an incorrect value. Not used for the I2S mode. RXCRC[31-16] bits are reserved at the peripheral instances with data size limited to 16-bit. There is no constrain when 32-bit access is applied at these addresses. Reserved bits 31-16 are always read zero while any write to them is ignored. Note: The configuration of CRCSIZE bit field is not taken into account when the content of this register is read by software. No masking is applied for unused bits at this case.
0x5001504C B  REGISTER UDRDR: SPI/I2S underrun data register
0x5001504C C   FIELD 00w32 UDRDR (rw): data at slave underrun condition The register is taken into account in Slave mode and at underrun condition only. The number of bits considered depends on DSIZE bit settings of the SPI_CFG1 register. Underrun condition handling depends on setting UDRCFG bit at SPI_CFG1 register. Note: UDRDR[31-16] bits are reserved at the peripheral instances with data size limited to 16-bit. There is no constraint when 32-bit access is applied at these addresses. Reserved bits 31-16 are always read zero while any write to them is ignored.
0x50015050 B  REGISTER I2SCFGR: SPI/I2S configuration register
0x50015050 C   FIELD 00w01 I2SMOD (rw): I2S mode selection
0x50015050 C   FIELD 01w03 I2SCFG (rw): I2S configuration mode others, not used
0x50015050 C   FIELD 04w02 I2SSTD (rw): I2S standard selection For more details on I2S standards, refer to
0x50015050 C   FIELD 07w01 PCMSYNC (rw): PCM frame synchronization
0x50015050 C   FIELD 08w02 DATLEN (rw): data length to be transferred
0x50015050 C   FIELD 10w01 CHLEN (rw): channel length (number of bits per audio channel)
0x50015050 C   FIELD 11w01 CKPOL (rw): serial audio clock polarity
0x50015050 C   FIELD 12w01 FIXCH (rw): fixed channel length in slave
0x50015050 C   FIELD 13w01 WSINV (rw): word select inversion This bit is used to invert the default polarity of WS signal. WS is LOW. In PCM mode the start of frame is indicated by a rising edge. WS is HIGH. In PCM mode the start of frame is indicated by a falling edge.
0x50015050 C   FIELD 14w01 DATFMT (rw): data format
0x50015050 C   FIELD 16w08 I2SDIV (rw): I2S linear prescaler I2SDIV can take any values except the value 1, when ODD is also equal to 1. Refer to for details
0x50015050 C   FIELD 24w01 ODD (rw): odd factor for the prescaler Refer to for details
0x50015050 C   FIELD 25w01 MCKOE (rw): master clock output enable
0x50015400 A PERIPHERAL SEC_SAI1
0x50015400 B  REGISTER GCR: SAI global configuration register
0x50015400 C   FIELD 00w02 SYNCIN (rw): Synchronization inputs These bits are set and cleared by software. Refer to for information on how to program this field. These bits must be set when both audio blocks (A and B) are disabled. They are meaningful if one of the two audio blocks is defined to operate in synchronous mode with an external SAI (SYNCEN[1:0] = 10 in SAI_ACR1 or in SAI_BCR1 registers).
0x50015400 C   FIELD 04w02 SYNCOUT (rw): Synchronization outputs These bits are set and cleared by software.
0x50015404 B  REGISTER ACR1: SAI configuration register 1
0x50015404 C   FIELD 00w02 MODE (rw): SAIx audio block mode These bits are set and cleared by software. They must be configured when SAIx audio block is disabled. Note: When the audio block is configured in SPDIF mode, the master transmitter mode is forced (MODE[1:0] = 00).
0x50015404 C   FIELD 02w02 PRTCFG (rw): Protocol configuration These bits are set and cleared by software. These bits have to be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015404 C   FIELD 05w03 DS (rw): Data size These bits are set and cleared by software. These bits are ignored when the SPDIF protocols are selected (bit PRTCFG[1:0]), because the frame and the data size are fixed in such case. When the companding mode is selected through COMP[1:0] bits, DS[1:0] are ignored since the data size is fixed to 8 bits by the algorithm. These bits must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015404 C   FIELD 08w01 LSBFIRST (rw): Least significant bit first This bit is set and cleared by software. It must be configured when the audio block is disabled. This bit has no meaning in AC'97 audio protocol since AC'97 data are always transferred with the MSB first. This bit has no meaning in SPDIF audio protocol since in SPDIF data are always transferred with LSB first.
0x50015404 C   FIELD 09w01 CKSTR (rw): Clock strobing edge This bit is set and cleared by software. It must be configured when the audio block is disabled. This bit has no meaning in SPDIF audio protocol.
0x50015404 C   FIELD 10w02 SYNCEN (rw): Synchronization enable These bits are set and cleared by software. They must be configured when the audio subblock is disabled. Note: The audio subblock should be configured as asynchronous when SPDIF mode is enabled.
0x50015404 C   FIELD 12w01 MONO (rw): Mono mode This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningful only when the number of slots is equal to 2. When the mono mode is selected, slot 0 data are duplicated on slot 1 when the audio block operates as a transmitter. In reception mode, the slot1 is discarded and only the data received from slot 0 are stored. Refer to for more details.
0x50015404 C   FIELD 13w01 OUTDRIV (rw): Output drive This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: This bit has to be set before enabling the audio block and after the audio block configuration.
0x50015404 C   FIELD 16w01 SAIEN (rw): Audio block enable This bit is set by software. To switch off the audio block, the application software must program this bit to 0 and poll the bit till it reads back 0, meaning that the block is completely disabled. Before setting this bit to 1, check that it is set to 0, otherwise the enable command is not taken into account. This bit allows controlling the state of the SAI audio block. If it is disabled when an audio frame transfer is ongoing, the ongoing transfer completes and the cell is fully disabled at the end of this audio frame transfer. Note: When the SAI block (A or B) is configured in master mode, the clock must be present on the SAI block input before setting SAIEN bit.
0x50015404 C   FIELD 17w01 DMAEN (rw): DMA enable This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: Since the audio block defaults to operate as a transmitter after reset, the MODE[1:0] bits must be configured before setting DMAEN to avoid a DMA request in receiver mode.
0x50015404 C   FIELD 19w01 NODIV (rw): No divider This bit is set and cleared by software.
0x50015404 C   FIELD 20w06 MCKDIV (rw): Master clock divider These bits are set and cleared by software. Otherwise, The master clock frequency is calculated according to the formula given in . These bits have no meaning when the audio block is slave. They have to be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015404 C   FIELD 26w01 OSR (rw): Oversampling ratio for master clock This bit is meaningful only when NODIV bit is set to 0.
0x50015404 C   FIELD 27w01 MCKEN (rw): Master clock generation enable
0x50015408 B  REGISTER ACR2: SAI configuration register 2
0x50015408 C   FIELD 00w03 FTH (rw): FIFO threshold. This bit is set and cleared by software.
0x50015408 C   FIELD 03w01 FFLUSH (wo): FIFO flush. This bit is set by software. It is always read as 0. This bit should be configured when the SAI is disabled.
0x50015408 C   FIELD 04w01 TRIS (rw): Tristate management on data line. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningful only if the audio block is configured as a transmitter. This bit is not used when the audio block is configured in SPDIF mode. It should be configured when SAI is disabled. Refer to for more details.
0x50015408 C   FIELD 05w01 MUTE (rw): Mute. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningful only when the audio block operates as a transmitter. The MUTE value is linked to value of MUTEVAL if the number of slots is lower or equal to 2, or equal to 0 if it is greater than 2. Refer to for more details. Note: This bit is meaningless and should not be used for SPDIF audio blocks.
0x50015408 C   FIELD 06w01 MUTEVAL (rw): Mute value. This bit is set and cleared by software.It must be written before enabling the audio block: SAIEN. This bit is meaningful only when the audio block operates as a transmitter, the number of slots is lower or equal to 2 and the MUTE bit is set. If more slots are declared, the bit value sent during the transmission in mute mode is equal to 0, whatever the value of MUTEVAL. if the number of slot is lower or equal to 2 and MUTEVAL = 1, the MUTE value transmitted for each slot is the one sent during the previous frame. Refer to for more details. Note: This bit is meaningless and should not be used for SPDIF audio blocks.
0x50015408 C   FIELD 07w06 MUTECNT (rw): Mute counter. These bits are set and cleared by software. They are used only in reception mode. The value set in these bits is compared to the number of consecutive mute frames detected in reception. When the number of mute frames is equal to this value, the flag MUTEDET is set and an interrupt is generated if bit MUTEDETIE is set. Refer to for more details.
0x50015408 C   FIELD 13w01 CPL (rw): Complement bit. This bit is set and cleared by software. It defines the type of complement to be used for companding mode Note: This bit has effect only when the companding mode is -Law algorithm or A-Law algorithm.
0x50015408 C   FIELD 14w02 COMP (rw): Companding mode. These bits are set and cleared by software. The -Law and the A-Law log are a part of the CCITT G.711 recommendation, the type of complement that is used depends on CPL bit. The data expansion or data compression are determined by the state of bit MODE[0]. The data compression is applied if the audio block is configured as a transmitter. The data expansion is automatically applied when the audio block is configured as a receiver. Refer to for more details. Note: Companding mode is applicable only when Free protocol mode is selected.
0x5001540C B  REGISTER AFRCR: SAI frame configuration register
0x5001540C C   FIELD 00w08 FRL (rw): Frame length. These bits are set and cleared by software. They define the audio frame length expressed in number of SCK clock cycles: the number of bits in the frame is equal to FRL[7:0] + 1. The minimum number of bits to transfer in an audio frame must be equal to 8, otherwise the audio block behaves in an unexpected way. This is the case when the data size is 8 bits and only one slot 0 is defined in NBSLOT[4:0] of SAI_xSLOTR register (NBSLOT[3:0] = 0000). In master mode, if the master clock (available on MCLK_x pin) is used, the frame length should be aligned with a number equal to a power of 2, ranging from 8 to 256. When the master clock is not used (NODIV = 1), it is recommended to program the frame length to an value ranging from 8 to 256. These bits are meaningless and are not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. They must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001540C C   FIELD 08w07 FSALL (rw): Frame synchronization active level length. These bits are set and cleared by software. They specify the length in number of bit clock (SCK) + 1 (FSALL[6:0] + 1) of the active level of the FS signal in the audio frame These bits are meaningless and are not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. They must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001540C C   FIELD 16w01 FSDEF (rw): Frame synchronization definition. This bit is set and cleared by software. When the bit is set, the number of slots defined in the SAI_xSLOTR register has to be even. It means that half of this number of slots are dedicated to the left channel and the other slots for the right channel (e.g: this bit has to be set for I2S or MSB/LSB-justified protocols...). This bit is meaningless and is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. It must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001540C C   FIELD 17w01 FSPOL (rw): Frame synchronization polarity. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is used to configure the level of the start of frame on the FS signal. It is meaningless and is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. This bit must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001540C C   FIELD 18w01 FSOFF (rw): Frame synchronization offset. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningless and is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. This bit must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015410 B  REGISTER ASLOTR: SAI slot register
0x50015410 C   FIELD 00w05 FBOFF (rw): First bit offset These bits are set and cleared by software. The value set in this bitfield defines the position of the first data transfer bit in the slot. It represents an offset value. In transmission mode, the bits outside the data field are forced to 0. In reception mode, the extra received bits are discarded. These bits must be set when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015410 C   FIELD 06w02 SLOTSZ (rw): Slot size This bits is set and cleared by software. The slot size must be higher or equal to the data size. If this condition is not respected, the behavior of the SAI is undetermined. Refer to for information on how to drive SD line. These bits must be set when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015410 C   FIELD 08w04 NBSLOT (rw): Number of slots in an audio frame. These bits are set and cleared by software. The value set in this bitfield represents the number of slots + 1 in the audio frame (including the number of inactive slots). The maximum number of slots is 16. The number of slots should be even if FSDEF bit in the SAI_xFRCR register is set. The number of slots must be configured when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015410 C   FIELD 16w16 SLOTEN (rw): Slot enable. These bits are set and cleared by software. Each SLOTEN bit corresponds to a slot position from 0 to 15 (maximum 16 slots). The slot must be enabled when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015414 B  REGISTER AIM: SAI interrupt mask register
0x50015414 C   FIELD 00w01 OVRUDRIE (rw): Overrun/underrun interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the OVRUDR bit in the SAI_xSR register is set.
0x50015414 C   FIELD 01w01 MUTEDETIE (rw): Mute detection interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the MUTEDET bit in the SAI_xSR register is set. This bit has a meaning only if the audio block is configured in receiver mode.
0x50015414 C   FIELD 02w01 WCKCFGIE (rw): Wrong clock configuration interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. This bit is taken into account only if the audio block is configured as a master (MODE[1] = 0) and NODIV = 0. It generates an interrupt if the WCKCFG flag in the SAI_xSR register is set. Note: This bit is used only in Free protocol mode and is meaningless in other modes.
0x50015414 C   FIELD 03w01 FREQIE (rw): FIFO request interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the FREQ bit in the SAI_xSR register is set. Since the audio block defaults to operate as a transmitter after reset, the MODE bit must be configured before setting FREQIE to avoid a parasitic interrupt in receiver mode,
0x50015414 C   FIELD 04w01 CNRDYIE (rw): Codec not ready interrupt enable (AC'97). This bit is set and cleared by software. When the interrupt is enabled, the audio block detects in the slot 0 (tag0) of the AC'97 frame if the Codec connected to this line is ready or not. If it is not ready, the CNRDY flag in the SAI_xSR register is set and an interrupt is generated. This bit has a meaning only if the AC'97 mode is selected through PRTCFG[1:0] bits and the audio block is operates as a receiver.
0x50015414 C   FIELD 05w01 AFSDETIE (rw): Anticipated frame synchronization detection interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the AFSDET bit in the SAI_xSR register is set. This bit is meaningless in AC'97, SPDIF mode or when the audio block operates as a master.
0x50015414 C   FIELD 06w01 LFSDETIE (rw): Late frame synchronization detection interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the LFSDET bit is set in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is meaningless in AC'97, SPDIF mode or when the audio block operates as a master.
0x50015418 B  REGISTER ASR: SAI status register
0x50015418 C   FIELD 00w01 OVRUDR (ro): Overrun / underrun. This bit is read only. The overrun and underrun conditions can occur only when the audio block is configured as a receiver and a transmitter, respectively. It can generate an interrupt if OVRUDRIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets COVRUDR bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015418 C   FIELD 01w01 MUTEDET (ro): Mute detection. This bit is read only. This flag is set if consecutive 0 values are received in each slot of a given audio frame and for a consecutive number of audio frames (set in the MUTECNT bit in the SAI_xCR2 register). It can generate an interrupt if MUTEDETIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets bit CMUTEDET in the SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015418 C   FIELD 02w01 WCKCFG (ro): Wrong clock configuration flag. This bit is read only. This bit is used only when the audio block operates in master mode (MODE[1] = 0) and NODIV = 0. It can generate an interrupt if WCKCFGIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets CWCKCFG bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015418 C   FIELD 03w01 FREQ (ro): FIFO request. This bit is read only. The request depends on the audio block configuration: If the block is configured in transmission mode, the FIFO request is related to a write request operation in the SAI_xDR. If the block configured in reception, the FIFO request related to a read request operation from the SAI_xDR. This flag can generate an interrupt if FREQIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register.
0x50015418 C   FIELD 04w01 CNRDY (ro): Codec not ready. This bit is read only. This bit is used only when the AC'97 audio protocol is selected in the SAI_xCR1 register and configured in receiver mode. It can generate an interrupt if CNRDYIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets CCNRDY bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015418 C   FIELD 05w01 AFSDET (ro): Anticipated frame synchronization detection. This bit is read only. This flag can be set only if the audio block is configured in slave mode. It is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF mode. It can generate an interrupt if AFSDETIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets CAFSDET bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015418 C   FIELD 06w01 LFSDET (ro): Late frame synchronization detection. This bit is read only. This flag can be set only if the audio block is configured in slave mode. It is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF mode. It can generate an interrupt if LFSDETIE bit is set in the SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets bit CLFSDET in SAI_xCLRFR register
0x50015418 C   FIELD 16w03 FLVL (ro): FIFO level threshold. This bit is read only. The FIFO level threshold flag is managed only by hardware and its setting depends on SAI block configuration (transmitter or receiver mode). Others: Reserved
0x5001541C B  REGISTER ACLRFR: SAI clear flag register
0x5001541C C   FIELD 00w01 COVRUDR (wo): Clear overrun / underrun. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the OVRUDR flag in the SAI_xSR register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001541C C   FIELD 01w01 CMUTEDET (wo): Mute detection flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the MUTEDET flag in the SAI_xSR register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001541C C   FIELD 02w01 CWCKCFG (wo): Clear wrong clock configuration flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the WCKCFG flag in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is used only when the audio block is set as master (MODE[1] = 0) and NODIV = 0 in the SAI_xCR1 register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001541C C   FIELD 04w01 CCNRDY (wo): Clear Codec not ready flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the CNRDY flag in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is used only when the AC'97 audio protocol is selected in the SAI_xCR1 register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001541C C   FIELD 05w01 CAFSDET (wo): Clear anticipated frame synchronization detection flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the AFSDET flag in the SAI_xSR register. It is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF mode. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001541C C   FIELD 06w01 CLFSDET (wo): Clear late frame synchronization detection flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the LFSDET flag in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF mode Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x50015420 B  REGISTER ADR: SAI data register
0x50015420 C   FIELD 00w32 DATA (rw): Data A write to this register loads the FIFO provided the FIFO is not full. A read from this register empties the FIFO if the FIFO is not empty.
0x50015424 B  REGISTER BCR1: SAI configuration register 1
0x50015424 C   FIELD 00w02 MODE (rw): SAIx audio block mode These bits are set and cleared by software. They must be configured when SAIx audio block is disabled. Note: When the audio block is configured in SPDIF mode, the master transmitter mode is forced (MODE[1:0] = 00). In Master transmitter mode, the audio block starts generating the FS and the clocks immediately.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 02w02 PRTCFG (rw): Protocol configuration These bits are set and cleared by software. These bits have to be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 05w03 DS (rw): Data size These bits are set and cleared by software. These bits are ignored when the SPDIF protocols are selected (bit PRTCFG[1:0]), because the frame and the data size are fixed in such case. When the companding mode is selected through COMP[1:0] bits, DS[1:0] are ignored since the data size is fixed to 8 bits by the algorithm. These bits must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 08w01 LSBFIRST (rw): Least significant bit first This bit is set and cleared by software. It must be configured when the audio block is disabled. This bit has no meaning in AC'97 audio protocol since AC'97 data are always transferred with the MSB first. This bit has no meaning in SPDIF audio protocol since in SPDIF data are always transferred with LSB first.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 09w01 CKSTR (rw): Clock strobing edge This bit is set and cleared by software. It must be configured when the audio block is disabled. This bit has no meaning in SPDIF audio protocol.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 10w02 SYNCEN (rw): Synchronization enable These bits are set and cleared by software. They must be configured when the audio subblock is disabled. Note: The audio subblock should be configured as asynchronous when SPDIF mode is enabled.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 12w01 MONO (rw): Mono mode This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningful only when the number of slots is equal to 2. When the mono mode is selected, slot 0 data are duplicated on slot 1 when the audio block operates as a transmitter. In reception mode, the slot1 is discarded and only the data received from slot 0 are stored. Refer to for more details.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 13w01 OUTDRIV (rw): Output drive This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: This bit has to be set before enabling the audio block and after the audio block configuration.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 16w01 SAIEN (rw): Audio block enable This bit is set by software. To switch off the audio block, the application software must program this bit to 0 and poll the bit till it reads back 0, meaning that the block is completely disabled. Before setting this bit to 1, check that it is set to 0, otherwise the enable command is not taken into account. This bit allows controlling the state of the SAI audio block. If it is disabled when an audio frame transfer is ongoing, the ongoing transfer completes and the cell is fully disabled at the end of this audio frame transfer. Note: When the SAI block (A or B) is configured in master mode, the clock must be present on the SAI block input before setting SAIEN bit.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 17w01 DMAEN (rw): DMA enable This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: Since the audio block defaults to operate as a transmitter after reset, the MODE[1:0] bits must be configured before setting DMAEN to avoid a DMA request in receiver mode.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 19w01 NODIV (rw): No divider This bit is set and cleared by software.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 20w06 MCKDIV (rw): Master clock divider These bits are set and cleared by software. Otherwise, The master clock frequency is calculated according to the formula given in . These bits have no meaning when the audio block is slave. They have to be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 26w01 OSR (rw): Oversampling ratio for master clock This bit is meaningful only when NODIV bit is set to 0.
0x50015424 C   FIELD 27w01 MCKEN (rw): Master clock generation enable
0x50015428 B  REGISTER BCR2: SAI configuration register 2
0x50015428 C   FIELD 00w03 FTH (rw): FIFO threshold. This bit is set and cleared by software.
0x50015428 C   FIELD 03w01 FFLUSH (wo): FIFO flush. This bit is set by software. It is always read as 0. This bit should be configured when the SAI is disabled.
0x50015428 C   FIELD 04w01 TRIS (rw): Tristate management on data line. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningful only if the audio block is configured as a transmitter. This bit is not used when the audio block is configured in SPDIF mode. It should be configured when SAI is disabled. Refer to for more details.
0x50015428 C   FIELD 05w01 MUTE (rw): Mute. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningful only when the audio block operates as a transmitter. The MUTE value is linked to value of MUTEVAL if the number of slots is lower or equal to 2, or equal to 0 if it is greater than 2. Refer to for more details. Note: This bit is meaningless and should not be used for SPDIF audio blocks.
0x50015428 C   FIELD 06w01 MUTEVAL (rw): Mute value. This bit is set and cleared by software.It must be written before enabling the audio block: SAIEN. This bit is meaningful only when the audio block operates as a transmitter, the number of slots is lower or equal to 2 and the MUTE bit is set. If more slots are declared, the bit value sent during the transmission in mute mode is equal to 0, whatever the value of MUTEVAL. if the number of slot is lower or equal to 2 and MUTEVAL = 1, the MUTE value transmitted for each slot is the one sent during the previous frame. Refer to for more details. Note: This bit is meaningless and should not be used for SPDIF audio blocks.
0x50015428 C   FIELD 07w06 MUTECNT (rw): Mute counter. These bits are set and cleared by software. They are used only in reception mode. The value set in these bits is compared to the number of consecutive mute frames detected in reception. When the number of mute frames is equal to this value, the flag MUTEDET is set and an interrupt is generated if bit MUTEDETIE is set. Refer to for more details.
0x50015428 C   FIELD 13w01 CPL (rw): Complement bit. This bit is set and cleared by software. It defines the type of complement to be used for companding mode Note: This bit has effect only when the companding mode is -Law algorithm or A-Law algorithm.
0x50015428 C   FIELD 14w02 COMP (rw): Companding mode. These bits are set and cleared by software. The -Law and the A-Law log are a part of the CCITT G.711 recommendation, the type of complement that is used depends on CPL bit. The data expansion or data compression are determined by the state of bit MODE[0]. The data compression is applied if the audio block is configured as a transmitter. The data expansion is automatically applied when the audio block is configured as a receiver. Refer to for more details. Note: Companding mode is applicable only when Free protocol mode is selected.
0x5001542C B  REGISTER BFRCR: SAI frame configuration register
0x5001542C C   FIELD 00w08 FRL (rw): Frame length. These bits are set and cleared by software. They define the audio frame length expressed in number of SCK clock cycles: the number of bits in the frame is equal to FRL[7:0] + 1. The minimum number of bits to transfer in an audio frame must be equal to 8, otherwise the audio block behaves in an unexpected way. This is the case when the data size is 8 bits and only one slot 0 is defined in NBSLOT[4:0] of SAI_xSLOTR register (NBSLOT[3:0] = 0000). In master mode, if the master clock (available on MCLK_x pin) is used, the frame length should be aligned with a number equal to a power of 2, ranging from 8 to 256. When the master clock is not used (NODIV = 1), it is recommended to program the frame length to an value ranging from 8 to 256. These bits are meaningless and are not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration.
0x5001542C C   FIELD 08w07 FSALL (rw): Frame synchronization active level length. These bits are set and cleared by software. They specify the length in number of bit clock (SCK) + 1 (FSALL[6:0] + 1) of the active level of the FS signal in the audio frame These bits are meaningless and are not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. They must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001542C C   FIELD 16w01 FSDEF (rw): Frame synchronization definition. This bit is set and cleared by software. When the bit is set, the number of slots defined in the SAI_xSLOTR register has to be even. It means that half of this number of slots is dedicated to the left channel and the other slots for the right channel (e.g: this bit has to be set for I2S or MSB/LSB-justified protocols...). This bit is meaningless and is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. It must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001542C C   FIELD 17w01 FSPOL (rw): Frame synchronization polarity. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is used to configure the level of the start of frame on the FS signal. It is meaningless and is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. This bit must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001542C C   FIELD 18w01 FSOFF (rw): Frame synchronization offset. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningless and is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. This bit must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015430 B  REGISTER BSLOTR: SAI slot register
0x50015430 C   FIELD 00w05 FBOFF (rw): First bit offset These bits are set and cleared by software. The value set in this bitfield defines the position of the first data transfer bit in the slot. It represents an offset value. In transmission mode, the bits outside the data field are forced to 0. In reception mode, the extra received bits are discarded. These bits must be set when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015430 C   FIELD 06w02 SLOTSZ (rw): Slot size This bits is set and cleared by software. The slot size must be higher or equal to the data size. If this condition is not respected, the behavior of the SAI is undetermined. Refer to for information on how to drive SD line. These bits must be set when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015430 C   FIELD 08w04 NBSLOT (rw): Number of slots in an audio frame. These bits are set and cleared by software. The value set in this bitfield represents the number of slots + 1 in the audio frame (including the number of inactive slots). The maximum number of slots is 16. The number of slots should be even if FSDEF bit in the SAI_xFRCR register is set. The number of slots must be configured when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015430 C   FIELD 16w16 SLOTEN (rw): Slot enable. These bits are set and cleared by software. Each SLOTEN bit corresponds to a slot position from 0 to 15 (maximum 16 slots). The slot must be enabled when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015434 B  REGISTER BIM: SAI interrupt mask register
0x50015434 C   FIELD 00w01 OVRUDRIE (rw): Overrun/underrun interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the OVRUDR bit in the SAI_xSR register is set.
0x50015434 C   FIELD 01w01 MUTEDETIE (rw): Mute detection interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the MUTEDET bit in the SAI_xSR register is set. This bit has a meaning only if the audio block is configured in receiver mode.
0x50015434 C   FIELD 02w01 WCKCFGIE (rw): Wrong clock configuration interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. This bit is taken into account only if the audio block is configured as a master (MODE[1] = 0) and NODIV = 0. It generates an interrupt if the WCKCFG flag in the SAI_xSR register is set. Note: This bit is used only in Free protocol mode and is meaningless in other modes.
0x50015434 C   FIELD 03w01 FREQIE (rw): FIFO request interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the FREQ bit in the SAI_xSR register is set. Since the audio block defaults to operate as a transmitter after reset, the MODE bit must be configured before setting FREQIE to avoid a parasitic interrupt in receiver mode,
0x50015434 C   FIELD 04w01 CNRDYIE (rw): Codec not ready interrupt enable (AC'97). This bit is set and cleared by software. When the interrupt is enabled, the audio block detects in the slot 0 (tag0) of the AC'97 frame if the Codec connected to this line is ready or not. If it is not ready, the CNRDY flag in the SAI_xSR register is set and an interrupt is generated. This bit has a meaning only if the AC'97 mode is selected through PRTCFG[1:0] bits and the audio block is operates as a receiver.
0x50015434 C   FIELD 05w01 AFSDETIE (rw): Anticipated frame synchronization detection interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the AFSDET bit in the SAI_xSR register is set. This bit is meaningless in AC'97, SPDIF mode or when the audio block operates as a master.
0x50015434 C   FIELD 06w01 LFSDETIE (rw): Late frame synchronization detection interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the LFSDET bit is set in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is meaningless in AC'97, SPDIF mode or when the audio block operates as a master.
0x50015438 B  REGISTER BSR: SAI status register
0x50015438 C   FIELD 00w01 OVRUDR (ro): Overrun / underrun. This bit is read only. The overrun and underrun conditions can occur only when the audio block is configured as a receiver and a transmitter, respectively. It can generate an interrupt if OVRUDRIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets COVRUDR bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015438 C   FIELD 01w01 MUTEDET (ro): Mute detection. This bit is read only. This flag is set if consecutive 0 values are received in each slot of a given audio frame and for a consecutive number of audio frames (set in the MUTECNT bit in the SAI_xCR2 register). It can generate an interrupt if MUTEDETIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets bit CMUTEDET in the SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015438 C   FIELD 02w01 WCKCFG (ro): Wrong clock configuration flag. This bit is read only. This bit is used only when the audio block operates in master mode (MODE[1] = 0) and NODIV = 0. It can generate an interrupt if WCKCFGIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets CWCKCFG bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015438 C   FIELD 03w01 FREQ (ro): FIFO request. This bit is read only. The request depends on the audio block configuration: If the block is configured in transmission mode, the FIFO request is related to a write request operation in the SAI_xDR. If the block configured in reception, the FIFO request related to a read request operation from the SAI_xDR. This flag can generate an interrupt if FREQIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register.
0x50015438 C   FIELD 04w01 CNRDY (ro): Codec not ready. This bit is read only. This bit is used only when the AC'97 audio protocol is selected in the SAI_xCR1 register and configured in receiver mode. It can generate an interrupt if CNRDYIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets CCNRDY bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015438 C   FIELD 05w01 AFSDET (ro): Anticipated frame synchronization detection. This bit is read only. This flag can be set only if the audio block is configured in slave mode. It is not used in AC'97or SPDIF mode. It can generate an interrupt if AFSDETIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets CAFSDET bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015438 C   FIELD 06w01 LFSDET (ro): Late frame synchronization detection. This bit is read only. This flag can be set only if the audio block is configured in slave mode. It is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF mode. It can generate an interrupt if LFSDETIE bit is set in the SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets bit CLFSDET in SAI_xCLRFR register
0x50015438 C   FIELD 16w03 FLVL (ro): FIFO level threshold. This bit is read only. The FIFO level threshold flag is managed only by hardware and its setting depends on SAI block configuration (transmitter or receiver mode). Others: Reserved
0x5001543C B  REGISTER BCLRFR: SAI clear flag register
0x5001543C C   FIELD 00w01 COVRUDR (wo): Clear overrun / underrun. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the OVRUDR flag in the SAI_xSR register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001543C C   FIELD 01w01 CMUTEDET (wo): Mute detection flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the MUTEDET flag in the SAI_xSR register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001543C C   FIELD 02w01 CWCKCFG (wo): Clear wrong clock configuration flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the WCKCFG flag in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is used only when the audio block is set as master (MODE[1] = 0) and NODIV = 0 in the SAI_xCR1 register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001543C C   FIELD 04w01 CCNRDY (wo): Clear Codec not ready flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the CNRDY flag in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is used only when the AC'97 audio protocol is selected in the SAI_xCR1 register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001543C C   FIELD 05w01 CAFSDET (wo): Clear anticipated frame synchronization detection flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the AFSDET flag in the SAI_xSR register. It is not used in AC'97or SPDIF mode. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001543C C   FIELD 06w01 CLFSDET (wo): Clear late frame synchronization detection flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the LFSDET flag in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is not used in AC'97or SPDIF mode Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x50015440 B  REGISTER BDR: SAI data register
0x50015440 C   FIELD 00w32 DATA (rw): Data A write to this register loads the FIFO provided the FIFO is not full. A read from this register empties the FIFO if the FIFO is not empty.
0x50015444 B  REGISTER PDMCR: SAI PDM control register
0x50015444 C   FIELD 00w01 PDMEN (rw): PDM enable This bit is set and cleared by software. This bit allows to control the state of the PDM interface block. Make sure that the SAI in already operating in TDM master mode before enabling the PDM interface.
0x50015444 C   FIELD 04w02 MICNBR (rw): Number of microphones This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: It is not recommended to configure this field when PDMEN = 1.* The complete set of data lines might not be available for all SAI instances. Refer to for details.
0x50015444 C   FIELD 08w01 CKEN1 (rw): Clock enable of bitstream clock number 1 This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: It is not recommended to configure this bit when PDMEN = 1. SAI_CK1 might not be available for all SAI instances. Refer to implementation for details.
0x50015444 C   FIELD 09w01 CKEN2 (rw): Clock enable of bitstream clock number 2 This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: It is not recommended to configure this bit when PDMEN = 1. SAI_CK2 might not be available for all SAI instances. Refer to implementation for details.
0x50015448 B  REGISTER PDMDLY: SAI PDM delay register
0x50015448 C   FIELD 00w03 DLYM1L (rw): Delay line adjust for first microphone of pair 1 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D1 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015448 C   FIELD 04w03 DLYM1R (rw): Delay line adjust for second microphone of pair 1 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D1 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015448 C   FIELD 08w03 DLYM2L (rw): Delay line for first microphone of pair 2 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D2 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015448 C   FIELD 12w03 DLYM2R (rw): Delay line for second microphone of pair 2 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D2 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015448 C   FIELD 16w03 DLYM3L (rw): Delay line for first microphone of pair 3 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D3 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015448 C   FIELD 20w03 DLYM3R (rw): Delay line for second microphone of pair 3 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D3 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015448 C   FIELD 24w03 DLYM4L (rw): Delay line for first microphone of pair 4 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D4 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015448 C   FIELD 28w03 DLYM4R (rw): Delay line for second microphone of pair 4 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D4 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015800 A PERIPHERAL SEC_SAI2
0x50015800 B  REGISTER GCR: SAI global configuration register
0x50015800 C   FIELD 00w02 SYNCIN (rw): Synchronization inputs These bits are set and cleared by software. Refer to for information on how to program this field. These bits must be set when both audio blocks (A and B) are disabled. They are meaningful if one of the two audio blocks is defined to operate in synchronous mode with an external SAI (SYNCEN[1:0] = 10 in SAI_ACR1 or in SAI_BCR1 registers).
0x50015800 C   FIELD 04w02 SYNCOUT (rw): Synchronization outputs These bits are set and cleared by software.
0x50015804 B  REGISTER ACR1: SAI configuration register 1
0x50015804 C   FIELD 00w02 MODE (rw): SAIx audio block mode These bits are set and cleared by software. They must be configured when SAIx audio block is disabled. Note: When the audio block is configured in SPDIF mode, the master transmitter mode is forced (MODE[1:0] = 00).
0x50015804 C   FIELD 02w02 PRTCFG (rw): Protocol configuration These bits are set and cleared by software. These bits have to be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015804 C   FIELD 05w03 DS (rw): Data size These bits are set and cleared by software. These bits are ignored when the SPDIF protocols are selected (bit PRTCFG[1:0]), because the frame and the data size are fixed in such case. When the companding mode is selected through COMP[1:0] bits, DS[1:0] are ignored since the data size is fixed to 8 bits by the algorithm. These bits must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015804 C   FIELD 08w01 LSBFIRST (rw): Least significant bit first This bit is set and cleared by software. It must be configured when the audio block is disabled. This bit has no meaning in AC'97 audio protocol since AC'97 data are always transferred with the MSB first. This bit has no meaning in SPDIF audio protocol since in SPDIF data are always transferred with LSB first.
0x50015804 C   FIELD 09w01 CKSTR (rw): Clock strobing edge This bit is set and cleared by software. It must be configured when the audio block is disabled. This bit has no meaning in SPDIF audio protocol.
0x50015804 C   FIELD 10w02 SYNCEN (rw): Synchronization enable These bits are set and cleared by software. They must be configured when the audio subblock is disabled. Note: The audio subblock should be configured as asynchronous when SPDIF mode is enabled.
0x50015804 C   FIELD 12w01 MONO (rw): Mono mode This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningful only when the number of slots is equal to 2. When the mono mode is selected, slot 0 data are duplicated on slot 1 when the audio block operates as a transmitter. In reception mode, the slot1 is discarded and only the data received from slot 0 are stored. Refer to for more details.
0x50015804 C   FIELD 13w01 OUTDRIV (rw): Output drive This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: This bit has to be set before enabling the audio block and after the audio block configuration.
0x50015804 C   FIELD 16w01 SAIEN (rw): Audio block enable This bit is set by software. To switch off the audio block, the application software must program this bit to 0 and poll the bit till it reads back 0, meaning that the block is completely disabled. Before setting this bit to 1, check that it is set to 0, otherwise the enable command is not taken into account. This bit allows controlling the state of the SAI audio block. If it is disabled when an audio frame transfer is ongoing, the ongoing transfer completes and the cell is fully disabled at the end of this audio frame transfer. Note: When the SAI block (A or B) is configured in master mode, the clock must be present on the SAI block input before setting SAIEN bit.
0x50015804 C   FIELD 17w01 DMAEN (rw): DMA enable This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: Since the audio block defaults to operate as a transmitter after reset, the MODE[1:0] bits must be configured before setting DMAEN to avoid a DMA request in receiver mode.
0x50015804 C   FIELD 19w01 NODIV (rw): No divider This bit is set and cleared by software.
0x50015804 C   FIELD 20w06 MCKDIV (rw): Master clock divider These bits are set and cleared by software. Otherwise, The master clock frequency is calculated according to the formula given in . These bits have no meaning when the audio block is slave. They have to be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015804 C   FIELD 26w01 OSR (rw): Oversampling ratio for master clock This bit is meaningful only when NODIV bit is set to 0.
0x50015804 C   FIELD 27w01 MCKEN (rw): Master clock generation enable
0x50015808 B  REGISTER ACR2: SAI configuration register 2
0x50015808 C   FIELD 00w03 FTH (rw): FIFO threshold. This bit is set and cleared by software.
0x50015808 C   FIELD 03w01 FFLUSH (wo): FIFO flush. This bit is set by software. It is always read as 0. This bit should be configured when the SAI is disabled.
0x50015808 C   FIELD 04w01 TRIS (rw): Tristate management on data line. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningful only if the audio block is configured as a transmitter. This bit is not used when the audio block is configured in SPDIF mode. It should be configured when SAI is disabled. Refer to for more details.
0x50015808 C   FIELD 05w01 MUTE (rw): Mute. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningful only when the audio block operates as a transmitter. The MUTE value is linked to value of MUTEVAL if the number of slots is lower or equal to 2, or equal to 0 if it is greater than 2. Refer to for more details. Note: This bit is meaningless and should not be used for SPDIF audio blocks.
0x50015808 C   FIELD 06w01 MUTEVAL (rw): Mute value. This bit is set and cleared by software.It must be written before enabling the audio block: SAIEN. This bit is meaningful only when the audio block operates as a transmitter, the number of slots is lower or equal to 2 and the MUTE bit is set. If more slots are declared, the bit value sent during the transmission in mute mode is equal to 0, whatever the value of MUTEVAL. if the number of slot is lower or equal to 2 and MUTEVAL = 1, the MUTE value transmitted for each slot is the one sent during the previous frame. Refer to for more details. Note: This bit is meaningless and should not be used for SPDIF audio blocks.
0x50015808 C   FIELD 07w06 MUTECNT (rw): Mute counter. These bits are set and cleared by software. They are used only in reception mode. The value set in these bits is compared to the number of consecutive mute frames detected in reception. When the number of mute frames is equal to this value, the flag MUTEDET is set and an interrupt is generated if bit MUTEDETIE is set. Refer to for more details.
0x50015808 C   FIELD 13w01 CPL (rw): Complement bit. This bit is set and cleared by software. It defines the type of complement to be used for companding mode Note: This bit has effect only when the companding mode is -Law algorithm or A-Law algorithm.
0x50015808 C   FIELD 14w02 COMP (rw): Companding mode. These bits are set and cleared by software. The -Law and the A-Law log are a part of the CCITT G.711 recommendation, the type of complement that is used depends on CPL bit. The data expansion or data compression are determined by the state of bit MODE[0]. The data compression is applied if the audio block is configured as a transmitter. The data expansion is automatically applied when the audio block is configured as a receiver. Refer to for more details. Note: Companding mode is applicable only when Free protocol mode is selected.
0x5001580C B  REGISTER AFRCR: SAI frame configuration register
0x5001580C C   FIELD 00w08 FRL (rw): Frame length. These bits are set and cleared by software. They define the audio frame length expressed in number of SCK clock cycles: the number of bits in the frame is equal to FRL[7:0] + 1. The minimum number of bits to transfer in an audio frame must be equal to 8, otherwise the audio block behaves in an unexpected way. This is the case when the data size is 8 bits and only one slot 0 is defined in NBSLOT[4:0] of SAI_xSLOTR register (NBSLOT[3:0] = 0000). In master mode, if the master clock (available on MCLK_x pin) is used, the frame length should be aligned with a number equal to a power of 2, ranging from 8 to 256. When the master clock is not used (NODIV = 1), it is recommended to program the frame length to an value ranging from 8 to 256. These bits are meaningless and are not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. They must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001580C C   FIELD 08w07 FSALL (rw): Frame synchronization active level length. These bits are set and cleared by software. They specify the length in number of bit clock (SCK) + 1 (FSALL[6:0] + 1) of the active level of the FS signal in the audio frame These bits are meaningless and are not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. They must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001580C C   FIELD 16w01 FSDEF (rw): Frame synchronization definition. This bit is set and cleared by software. When the bit is set, the number of slots defined in the SAI_xSLOTR register has to be even. It means that half of this number of slots are dedicated to the left channel and the other slots for the right channel (e.g: this bit has to be set for I2S or MSB/LSB-justified protocols...). This bit is meaningless and is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. It must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001580C C   FIELD 17w01 FSPOL (rw): Frame synchronization polarity. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is used to configure the level of the start of frame on the FS signal. It is meaningless and is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. This bit must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001580C C   FIELD 18w01 FSOFF (rw): Frame synchronization offset. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningless and is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. This bit must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015810 B  REGISTER ASLOTR: SAI slot register
0x50015810 C   FIELD 00w05 FBOFF (rw): First bit offset These bits are set and cleared by software. The value set in this bitfield defines the position of the first data transfer bit in the slot. It represents an offset value. In transmission mode, the bits outside the data field are forced to 0. In reception mode, the extra received bits are discarded. These bits must be set when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015810 C   FIELD 06w02 SLOTSZ (rw): Slot size This bits is set and cleared by software. The slot size must be higher or equal to the data size. If this condition is not respected, the behavior of the SAI is undetermined. Refer to for information on how to drive SD line. These bits must be set when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015810 C   FIELD 08w04 NBSLOT (rw): Number of slots in an audio frame. These bits are set and cleared by software. The value set in this bitfield represents the number of slots + 1 in the audio frame (including the number of inactive slots). The maximum number of slots is 16. The number of slots should be even if FSDEF bit in the SAI_xFRCR register is set. The number of slots must be configured when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015810 C   FIELD 16w16 SLOTEN (rw): Slot enable. These bits are set and cleared by software. Each SLOTEN bit corresponds to a slot position from 0 to 15 (maximum 16 slots). The slot must be enabled when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015814 B  REGISTER AIM: SAI interrupt mask register
0x50015814 C   FIELD 00w01 OVRUDRIE (rw): Overrun/underrun interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the OVRUDR bit in the SAI_xSR register is set.
0x50015814 C   FIELD 01w01 MUTEDETIE (rw): Mute detection interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the MUTEDET bit in the SAI_xSR register is set. This bit has a meaning only if the audio block is configured in receiver mode.
0x50015814 C   FIELD 02w01 WCKCFGIE (rw): Wrong clock configuration interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. This bit is taken into account only if the audio block is configured as a master (MODE[1] = 0) and NODIV = 0. It generates an interrupt if the WCKCFG flag in the SAI_xSR register is set. Note: This bit is used only in Free protocol mode and is meaningless in other modes.
0x50015814 C   FIELD 03w01 FREQIE (rw): FIFO request interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the FREQ bit in the SAI_xSR register is set. Since the audio block defaults to operate as a transmitter after reset, the MODE bit must be configured before setting FREQIE to avoid a parasitic interrupt in receiver mode,
0x50015814 C   FIELD 04w01 CNRDYIE (rw): Codec not ready interrupt enable (AC'97). This bit is set and cleared by software. When the interrupt is enabled, the audio block detects in the slot 0 (tag0) of the AC'97 frame if the Codec connected to this line is ready or not. If it is not ready, the CNRDY flag in the SAI_xSR register is set and an interrupt is generated. This bit has a meaning only if the AC'97 mode is selected through PRTCFG[1:0] bits and the audio block is operates as a receiver.
0x50015814 C   FIELD 05w01 AFSDETIE (rw): Anticipated frame synchronization detection interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the AFSDET bit in the SAI_xSR register is set. This bit is meaningless in AC'97, SPDIF mode or when the audio block operates as a master.
0x50015814 C   FIELD 06w01 LFSDETIE (rw): Late frame synchronization detection interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the LFSDET bit is set in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is meaningless in AC'97, SPDIF mode or when the audio block operates as a master.
0x50015818 B  REGISTER ASR: SAI status register
0x50015818 C   FIELD 00w01 OVRUDR (ro): Overrun / underrun. This bit is read only. The overrun and underrun conditions can occur only when the audio block is configured as a receiver and a transmitter, respectively. It can generate an interrupt if OVRUDRIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets COVRUDR bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015818 C   FIELD 01w01 MUTEDET (ro): Mute detection. This bit is read only. This flag is set if consecutive 0 values are received in each slot of a given audio frame and for a consecutive number of audio frames (set in the MUTECNT bit in the SAI_xCR2 register). It can generate an interrupt if MUTEDETIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets bit CMUTEDET in the SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015818 C   FIELD 02w01 WCKCFG (ro): Wrong clock configuration flag. This bit is read only. This bit is used only when the audio block operates in master mode (MODE[1] = 0) and NODIV = 0. It can generate an interrupt if WCKCFGIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets CWCKCFG bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015818 C   FIELD 03w01 FREQ (ro): FIFO request. This bit is read only. The request depends on the audio block configuration: If the block is configured in transmission mode, the FIFO request is related to a write request operation in the SAI_xDR. If the block configured in reception, the FIFO request related to a read request operation from the SAI_xDR. This flag can generate an interrupt if FREQIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register.
0x50015818 C   FIELD 04w01 CNRDY (ro): Codec not ready. This bit is read only. This bit is used only when the AC'97 audio protocol is selected in the SAI_xCR1 register and configured in receiver mode. It can generate an interrupt if CNRDYIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets CCNRDY bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015818 C   FIELD 05w01 AFSDET (ro): Anticipated frame synchronization detection. This bit is read only. This flag can be set only if the audio block is configured in slave mode. It is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF mode. It can generate an interrupt if AFSDETIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets CAFSDET bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015818 C   FIELD 06w01 LFSDET (ro): Late frame synchronization detection. This bit is read only. This flag can be set only if the audio block is configured in slave mode. It is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF mode. It can generate an interrupt if LFSDETIE bit is set in the SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets bit CLFSDET in SAI_xCLRFR register
0x50015818 C   FIELD 16w03 FLVL (ro): FIFO level threshold. This bit is read only. The FIFO level threshold flag is managed only by hardware and its setting depends on SAI block configuration (transmitter or receiver mode). Others: Reserved
0x5001581C B  REGISTER ACLRFR: SAI clear flag register
0x5001581C C   FIELD 00w01 COVRUDR (wo): Clear overrun / underrun. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the OVRUDR flag in the SAI_xSR register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001581C C   FIELD 01w01 CMUTEDET (wo): Mute detection flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the MUTEDET flag in the SAI_xSR register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001581C C   FIELD 02w01 CWCKCFG (wo): Clear wrong clock configuration flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the WCKCFG flag in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is used only when the audio block is set as master (MODE[1] = 0) and NODIV = 0 in the SAI_xCR1 register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001581C C   FIELD 04w01 CCNRDY (wo): Clear Codec not ready flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the CNRDY flag in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is used only when the AC'97 audio protocol is selected in the SAI_xCR1 register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001581C C   FIELD 05w01 CAFSDET (wo): Clear anticipated frame synchronization detection flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the AFSDET flag in the SAI_xSR register. It is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF mode. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001581C C   FIELD 06w01 CLFSDET (wo): Clear late frame synchronization detection flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the LFSDET flag in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF mode Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x50015820 B  REGISTER ADR: SAI data register
0x50015820 C   FIELD 00w32 DATA (rw): Data A write to this register loads the FIFO provided the FIFO is not full. A read from this register empties the FIFO if the FIFO is not empty.
0x50015824 B  REGISTER BCR1: SAI configuration register 1
0x50015824 C   FIELD 00w02 MODE (rw): SAIx audio block mode These bits are set and cleared by software. They must be configured when SAIx audio block is disabled. Note: When the audio block is configured in SPDIF mode, the master transmitter mode is forced (MODE[1:0] = 00). In Master transmitter mode, the audio block starts generating the FS and the clocks immediately.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 02w02 PRTCFG (rw): Protocol configuration These bits are set and cleared by software. These bits have to be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 05w03 DS (rw): Data size These bits are set and cleared by software. These bits are ignored when the SPDIF protocols are selected (bit PRTCFG[1:0]), because the frame and the data size are fixed in such case. When the companding mode is selected through COMP[1:0] bits, DS[1:0] are ignored since the data size is fixed to 8 bits by the algorithm. These bits must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 08w01 LSBFIRST (rw): Least significant bit first This bit is set and cleared by software. It must be configured when the audio block is disabled. This bit has no meaning in AC'97 audio protocol since AC'97 data are always transferred with the MSB first. This bit has no meaning in SPDIF audio protocol since in SPDIF data are always transferred with LSB first.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 09w01 CKSTR (rw): Clock strobing edge This bit is set and cleared by software. It must be configured when the audio block is disabled. This bit has no meaning in SPDIF audio protocol.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 10w02 SYNCEN (rw): Synchronization enable These bits are set and cleared by software. They must be configured when the audio subblock is disabled. Note: The audio subblock should be configured as asynchronous when SPDIF mode is enabled.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 12w01 MONO (rw): Mono mode This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningful only when the number of slots is equal to 2. When the mono mode is selected, slot 0 data are duplicated on slot 1 when the audio block operates as a transmitter. In reception mode, the slot1 is discarded and only the data received from slot 0 are stored. Refer to for more details.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 13w01 OUTDRIV (rw): Output drive This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: This bit has to be set before enabling the audio block and after the audio block configuration.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 16w01 SAIEN (rw): Audio block enable This bit is set by software. To switch off the audio block, the application software must program this bit to 0 and poll the bit till it reads back 0, meaning that the block is completely disabled. Before setting this bit to 1, check that it is set to 0, otherwise the enable command is not taken into account. This bit allows controlling the state of the SAI audio block. If it is disabled when an audio frame transfer is ongoing, the ongoing transfer completes and the cell is fully disabled at the end of this audio frame transfer. Note: When the SAI block (A or B) is configured in master mode, the clock must be present on the SAI block input before setting SAIEN bit.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 17w01 DMAEN (rw): DMA enable This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: Since the audio block defaults to operate as a transmitter after reset, the MODE[1:0] bits must be configured before setting DMAEN to avoid a DMA request in receiver mode.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 19w01 NODIV (rw): No divider This bit is set and cleared by software.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 20w06 MCKDIV (rw): Master clock divider These bits are set and cleared by software. Otherwise, The master clock frequency is calculated according to the formula given in . These bits have no meaning when the audio block is slave. They have to be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 26w01 OSR (rw): Oversampling ratio for master clock This bit is meaningful only when NODIV bit is set to 0.
0x50015824 C   FIELD 27w01 MCKEN (rw): Master clock generation enable
0x50015828 B  REGISTER BCR2: SAI configuration register 2
0x50015828 C   FIELD 00w03 FTH (rw): FIFO threshold. This bit is set and cleared by software.
0x50015828 C   FIELD 03w01 FFLUSH (wo): FIFO flush. This bit is set by software. It is always read as 0. This bit should be configured when the SAI is disabled.
0x50015828 C   FIELD 04w01 TRIS (rw): Tristate management on data line. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningful only if the audio block is configured as a transmitter. This bit is not used when the audio block is configured in SPDIF mode. It should be configured when SAI is disabled. Refer to for more details.
0x50015828 C   FIELD 05w01 MUTE (rw): Mute. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningful only when the audio block operates as a transmitter. The MUTE value is linked to value of MUTEVAL if the number of slots is lower or equal to 2, or equal to 0 if it is greater than 2. Refer to for more details. Note: This bit is meaningless and should not be used for SPDIF audio blocks.
0x50015828 C   FIELD 06w01 MUTEVAL (rw): Mute value. This bit is set and cleared by software.It must be written before enabling the audio block: SAIEN. This bit is meaningful only when the audio block operates as a transmitter, the number of slots is lower or equal to 2 and the MUTE bit is set. If more slots are declared, the bit value sent during the transmission in mute mode is equal to 0, whatever the value of MUTEVAL. if the number of slot is lower or equal to 2 and MUTEVAL = 1, the MUTE value transmitted for each slot is the one sent during the previous frame. Refer to for more details. Note: This bit is meaningless and should not be used for SPDIF audio blocks.
0x50015828 C   FIELD 07w06 MUTECNT (rw): Mute counter. These bits are set and cleared by software. They are used only in reception mode. The value set in these bits is compared to the number of consecutive mute frames detected in reception. When the number of mute frames is equal to this value, the flag MUTEDET is set and an interrupt is generated if bit MUTEDETIE is set. Refer to for more details.
0x50015828 C   FIELD 13w01 CPL (rw): Complement bit. This bit is set and cleared by software. It defines the type of complement to be used for companding mode Note: This bit has effect only when the companding mode is -Law algorithm or A-Law algorithm.
0x50015828 C   FIELD 14w02 COMP (rw): Companding mode. These bits are set and cleared by software. The -Law and the A-Law log are a part of the CCITT G.711 recommendation, the type of complement that is used depends on CPL bit. The data expansion or data compression are determined by the state of bit MODE[0]. The data compression is applied if the audio block is configured as a transmitter. The data expansion is automatically applied when the audio block is configured as a receiver. Refer to for more details. Note: Companding mode is applicable only when Free protocol mode is selected.
0x5001582C B  REGISTER BFRCR: SAI frame configuration register
0x5001582C C   FIELD 00w08 FRL (rw): Frame length. These bits are set and cleared by software. They define the audio frame length expressed in number of SCK clock cycles: the number of bits in the frame is equal to FRL[7:0] + 1. The minimum number of bits to transfer in an audio frame must be equal to 8, otherwise the audio block behaves in an unexpected way. This is the case when the data size is 8 bits and only one slot 0 is defined in NBSLOT[4:0] of SAI_xSLOTR register (NBSLOT[3:0] = 0000). In master mode, if the master clock (available on MCLK_x pin) is used, the frame length should be aligned with a number equal to a power of 2, ranging from 8 to 256. When the master clock is not used (NODIV = 1), it is recommended to program the frame length to an value ranging from 8 to 256. These bits are meaningless and are not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration.
0x5001582C C   FIELD 08w07 FSALL (rw): Frame synchronization active level length. These bits are set and cleared by software. They specify the length in number of bit clock (SCK) + 1 (FSALL[6:0] + 1) of the active level of the FS signal in the audio frame These bits are meaningless and are not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. They must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001582C C   FIELD 16w01 FSDEF (rw): Frame synchronization definition. This bit is set and cleared by software. When the bit is set, the number of slots defined in the SAI_xSLOTR register has to be even. It means that half of this number of slots is dedicated to the left channel and the other slots for the right channel (e.g: this bit has to be set for I2S or MSB/LSB-justified protocols...). This bit is meaningless and is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. It must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001582C C   FIELD 17w01 FSPOL (rw): Frame synchronization polarity. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is used to configure the level of the start of frame on the FS signal. It is meaningless and is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. This bit must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x5001582C C   FIELD 18w01 FSOFF (rw): Frame synchronization offset. This bit is set and cleared by software. It is meaningless and is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF audio block configuration. This bit must be configured when the audio block is disabled.
0x50015830 B  REGISTER BSLOTR: SAI slot register
0x50015830 C   FIELD 00w05 FBOFF (rw): First bit offset These bits are set and cleared by software. The value set in this bitfield defines the position of the first data transfer bit in the slot. It represents an offset value. In transmission mode, the bits outside the data field are forced to 0. In reception mode, the extra received bits are discarded. These bits must be set when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015830 C   FIELD 06w02 SLOTSZ (rw): Slot size This bits is set and cleared by software. The slot size must be higher or equal to the data size. If this condition is not respected, the behavior of the SAI is undetermined. Refer to for information on how to drive SD line. These bits must be set when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015830 C   FIELD 08w04 NBSLOT (rw): Number of slots in an audio frame. These bits are set and cleared by software. The value set in this bitfield represents the number of slots + 1 in the audio frame (including the number of inactive slots). The maximum number of slots is 16. The number of slots should be even if FSDEF bit in the SAI_xFRCR register is set. The number of slots must be configured when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015830 C   FIELD 16w16 SLOTEN (rw): Slot enable. These bits are set and cleared by software. Each SLOTEN bit corresponds to a slot position from 0 to 15 (maximum 16 slots). The slot must be enabled when the audio block is disabled. They are ignored in AC'97 or SPDIF mode.
0x50015834 B  REGISTER BIM: SAI interrupt mask register
0x50015834 C   FIELD 00w01 OVRUDRIE (rw): Overrun/underrun interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the OVRUDR bit in the SAI_xSR register is set.
0x50015834 C   FIELD 01w01 MUTEDETIE (rw): Mute detection interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the MUTEDET bit in the SAI_xSR register is set. This bit has a meaning only if the audio block is configured in receiver mode.
0x50015834 C   FIELD 02w01 WCKCFGIE (rw): Wrong clock configuration interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. This bit is taken into account only if the audio block is configured as a master (MODE[1] = 0) and NODIV = 0. It generates an interrupt if the WCKCFG flag in the SAI_xSR register is set. Note: This bit is used only in Free protocol mode and is meaningless in other modes.
0x50015834 C   FIELD 03w01 FREQIE (rw): FIFO request interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the FREQ bit in the SAI_xSR register is set. Since the audio block defaults to operate as a transmitter after reset, the MODE bit must be configured before setting FREQIE to avoid a parasitic interrupt in receiver mode,
0x50015834 C   FIELD 04w01 CNRDYIE (rw): Codec not ready interrupt enable (AC'97). This bit is set and cleared by software. When the interrupt is enabled, the audio block detects in the slot 0 (tag0) of the AC'97 frame if the Codec connected to this line is ready or not. If it is not ready, the CNRDY flag in the SAI_xSR register is set and an interrupt is generated. This bit has a meaning only if the AC'97 mode is selected through PRTCFG[1:0] bits and the audio block is operates as a receiver.
0x50015834 C   FIELD 05w01 AFSDETIE (rw): Anticipated frame synchronization detection interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the AFSDET bit in the SAI_xSR register is set. This bit is meaningless in AC'97, SPDIF mode or when the audio block operates as a master.
0x50015834 C   FIELD 06w01 LFSDETIE (rw): Late frame synchronization detection interrupt enable. This bit is set and cleared by software. When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated if the LFSDET bit is set in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is meaningless in AC'97, SPDIF mode or when the audio block operates as a master.
0x50015838 B  REGISTER BSR: SAI status register
0x50015838 C   FIELD 00w01 OVRUDR (ro): Overrun / underrun. This bit is read only. The overrun and underrun conditions can occur only when the audio block is configured as a receiver and a transmitter, respectively. It can generate an interrupt if OVRUDRIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets COVRUDR bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015838 C   FIELD 01w01 MUTEDET (ro): Mute detection. This bit is read only. This flag is set if consecutive 0 values are received in each slot of a given audio frame and for a consecutive number of audio frames (set in the MUTECNT bit in the SAI_xCR2 register). It can generate an interrupt if MUTEDETIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets bit CMUTEDET in the SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015838 C   FIELD 02w01 WCKCFG (ro): Wrong clock configuration flag. This bit is read only. This bit is used only when the audio block operates in master mode (MODE[1] = 0) and NODIV = 0. It can generate an interrupt if WCKCFGIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets CWCKCFG bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015838 C   FIELD 03w01 FREQ (ro): FIFO request. This bit is read only. The request depends on the audio block configuration: If the block is configured in transmission mode, the FIFO request is related to a write request operation in the SAI_xDR. If the block configured in reception, the FIFO request related to a read request operation from the SAI_xDR. This flag can generate an interrupt if FREQIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register.
0x50015838 C   FIELD 04w01 CNRDY (ro): Codec not ready. This bit is read only. This bit is used only when the AC'97 audio protocol is selected in the SAI_xCR1 register and configured in receiver mode. It can generate an interrupt if CNRDYIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets CCNRDY bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015838 C   FIELD 05w01 AFSDET (ro): Anticipated frame synchronization detection. This bit is read only. This flag can be set only if the audio block is configured in slave mode. It is not used in AC'97or SPDIF mode. It can generate an interrupt if AFSDETIE bit is set in SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets CAFSDET bit in SAI_xCLRFR register.
0x50015838 C   FIELD 06w01 LFSDET (ro): Late frame synchronization detection. This bit is read only. This flag can be set only if the audio block is configured in slave mode. It is not used in AC'97 or SPDIF mode. It can generate an interrupt if LFSDETIE bit is set in the SAI_xIM register. This flag is cleared when the software sets bit CLFSDET in SAI_xCLRFR register
0x50015838 C   FIELD 16w03 FLVL (ro): FIFO level threshold. This bit is read only. The FIFO level threshold flag is managed only by hardware and its setting depends on SAI block configuration (transmitter or receiver mode). Others: Reserved
0x5001583C B  REGISTER BCLRFR: SAI clear flag register
0x5001583C C   FIELD 00w01 COVRUDR (wo): Clear overrun / underrun. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the OVRUDR flag in the SAI_xSR register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001583C C   FIELD 01w01 CMUTEDET (wo): Mute detection flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the MUTEDET flag in the SAI_xSR register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001583C C   FIELD 02w01 CWCKCFG (wo): Clear wrong clock configuration flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the WCKCFG flag in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is used only when the audio block is set as master (MODE[1] = 0) and NODIV = 0 in the SAI_xCR1 register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001583C C   FIELD 04w01 CCNRDY (wo): Clear Codec not ready flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the CNRDY flag in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is used only when the AC'97 audio protocol is selected in the SAI_xCR1 register. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001583C C   FIELD 05w01 CAFSDET (wo): Clear anticipated frame synchronization detection flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the AFSDET flag in the SAI_xSR register. It is not used in AC'97or SPDIF mode. Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x5001583C C   FIELD 06w01 CLFSDET (wo): Clear late frame synchronization detection flag. This bit is write only. Programming this bit to 1 clears the LFSDET flag in the SAI_xSR register. This bit is not used in AC'97or SPDIF mode Reading this bit always returns the value 0.
0x50015840 B  REGISTER BDR: SAI data register
0x50015840 C   FIELD 00w32 DATA (rw): Data A write to this register loads the FIFO provided the FIFO is not full. A read from this register empties the FIFO if the FIFO is not empty.
0x50015844 B  REGISTER PDMCR: SAI PDM control register
0x50015844 C   FIELD 00w01 PDMEN (rw): PDM enable This bit is set and cleared by software. This bit allows to control the state of the PDM interface block. Make sure that the SAI in already operating in TDM master mode before enabling the PDM interface.
0x50015844 C   FIELD 04w02 MICNBR (rw): Number of microphones This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: It is not recommended to configure this field when PDMEN = 1.* The complete set of data lines might not be available for all SAI instances. Refer to for details.
0x50015844 C   FIELD 08w01 CKEN1 (rw): Clock enable of bitstream clock number 1 This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: It is not recommended to configure this bit when PDMEN = 1. SAI_CK1 might not be available for all SAI instances. Refer to implementation for details.
0x50015844 C   FIELD 09w01 CKEN2 (rw): Clock enable of bitstream clock number 2 This bit is set and cleared by software. Note: It is not recommended to configure this bit when PDMEN = 1. SAI_CK2 might not be available for all SAI instances. Refer to implementation for details.
0x50015848 B  REGISTER PDMDLY: SAI PDM delay register
0x50015848 C   FIELD 00w03 DLYM1L (rw): Delay line adjust for first microphone of pair 1 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D1 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015848 C   FIELD 04w03 DLYM1R (rw): Delay line adjust for second microphone of pair 1 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D1 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015848 C   FIELD 08w03 DLYM2L (rw): Delay line for first microphone of pair 2 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D2 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015848 C   FIELD 12w03 DLYM2R (rw): Delay line for second microphone of pair 2 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D2 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015848 C   FIELD 16w03 DLYM3L (rw): Delay line for first microphone of pair 3 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D3 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015848 C   FIELD 20w03 DLYM3R (rw): Delay line for second microphone of pair 3 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D3 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015848 C   FIELD 24w03 DLYM4L (rw): Delay line for first microphone of pair 4 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D4 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50015848 C   FIELD 28w03 DLYM4R (rw): Delay line for second microphone of pair 4 This bit is set and cleared by software. ... This field can be changed on-the-fly. Note: This field can be used only if D4 line is available.Refer to to check if it is available.
0x50016000 A PERIPHERAL SEC_USB
0x50016000 B  REGISTER CHEP0R: USB endpoint/channel 0 register
0x50016000 C   FIELD 00w04 EA (rw): endpoint/channel address Device mode Software must write in this field the 4-bit address used to identify the transactions directed to this endpoint. A value must be written before enabling the corresponding endpoint. Host mode Software must write in this field the 4-bit address used to identify the channel addressed by the host transaction.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 04w02 STATTX (rw): Status bits, for transmission transfers Device mode These bits contain the information about the endpoint status, listed in . These bits can be toggled by the software to initialize their value. When the application software writes 0, the value remains unchanged, while writing 1 makes the bit value to toggle. Hardware sets the STATTX bits to NAK, when a correct transfer has occurred (VTTX = 1) corresponding to a IN or SETUP (control only) transaction addressed to this channel/endpoint. It then waits for the software to prepare the next set of data to be transmitted. Double-buffered bulk endpoints implement a special transaction flow control, which controls the status based on buffer availability condition (Refer to endpoints and usage in Device mode). If the endpoint is defined as isochronous, its status can only be 'VALID' or 'DISABLED'. Therefore, the hardware cannot change the status of the channel/endpoint/channel after a successful transaction. If the software sets the STATTX bits to 'STALL' or 'NAK' for an isochronous channel/endpoint, the USB peripheral behavior is not defined. These bits are read/write but they can be only toggled by writing 1. Host mode The STATTX bits contain the information about the channel status. Refer to for the full descriptions ('Host mode' descriptions). Whereas in Device mode, these bits contain the status that are given out on the following transaction, in Host mode they capture the status last received from the device. If a NAK is received, STATTX contains the value indicating NAK.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 06w01 DTOGTX (rw): Data toggle, for transmission transfers If the endpoint/channel is non-isochronous, this bit contains the required value of the data toggle bit (0 = DATA0, 1 = DATA1) for the next data packet to be transmitted. Hardware toggles this bit when the ACK handshake is received from the USB host, following a data packet transmission. If the endpoint/channel is defined as a control one, hardware sets this bit to 1 at the reception of a SETUP PID addressed to this endpoint. If the endpoint/channel is using the double buffer feature, this bit is used to support packet buffer swapping too (Refer to Device mode) If the endpoint/channel is isochronous, this bit is used to support packet buffer swapping since no data toggling is used for this sort of endpoints and only DATA0 packet are transmitted (refer to ). Hardware toggles this bit just after the end of data packet transmission, since no handshake is used for isochronous transfers. This bit can also be toggled by the software to initialize its value (mandatory when the endpoint/channel is not a control one) or to force a specific data toggle/packet buffer usage. When the application software writes 0, the value of DTOGTX remains unchanged, while writing 1 makes the bit value to toggle. This bit is read/write but it can only be toggled by writing 1.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 07w01 VTTX (rw): Valid USB transaction transmitted Device mode This bit is set by the hardware when an IN transaction is successfully completed on this endpoint; the software can only clear this bit. If the CTRM bit in the USB_CNTR register is set accordingly, a generic interrupt condition is generated together with the endpoint related interrupt condition, which is always activated. A transaction ended with a NAK or STALL handshake does not set this bit, since no data is actually transferred, as in the case of protocol errors or data toggle mismatches. This bit is read/write but only 0 can be written. Host mode Same as VTRX behavior but for USB OUT and SETUP transactions.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 08w01 EPKIND (rw): endpoint/channel kind The meaning of this bit depends on the endpoint/channel type configured by the UTYPE bits. summarizes the different meanings. DBL_BUF: This bit is set by the software to enable the double-buffering feature for this bulk endpoint. The usage of double-buffered bulk endpoints is explained in Double-buffered endpoints and usage in Device mode. STATUS_OUT: This bit is set by the software to indicate that a status out transaction is expected: in this case all OUT transactions containing more than zero data bytes are answered 'STALL' instead of 'ACK'. This bit may be used to improve the robustness of the application to protocol errors during control transfers and its usage is intended for control endpoints only. When STATUS_OUT is reset, OUT transactions can have any number of bytes, as required.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 09w02 UTYPE (rw): USB type of transaction These bits configure the behavior of this endpoint/channel as described in Endpoint/channel type encoding. Channel0/Endpoint0 must always be a control endpoint/channel and each USB function must have at least one control endpoint/channel which has address 0, but there may be other control channels/endpoints if required. Only control channels/endpoints handle SETUP transactions, which are ignored by endpoints of other kinds. SETUP transactions cannot be answered with NAK or STALL. If a control endpoint/channel is defined as NAK, the USB peripheral does not answer, simulating a receive error, in the receive direction when a SETUP transaction is received. If the control endpoint/channel is defined as STALL in the receive direction, then the SETUP packet is accepted anyway, transferring data and issuing the CTR interrupt. The reception of OUT transactions is handled in the normal way, even if the endpoint/channel is a control one. Bulk and interrupt endpoints have very similar behavior and they differ only in the special feature available using the EPKIND configuration bit. The usage of isochronous channels/endpoints is explained in transfers in Device mode
0x50016000 C   FIELD 11w01 SETUP (ro): Setup transaction completed Device mode This bit is read-only and it is set by the hardware when the last completed transaction is a SETUP. This bit changes its value only for control endpoints. It must be examined, in the case of a successful receive transaction (VTRX event), to determine the type of transaction occurred. To protect the interrupt service routine from the changes in SETUP bits due to next incoming tokens, this bit is kept frozen while VTRX bit is at 1; its state changes when VTRX is at 0. This bit is read-only. Host mode This bit is set by the software to send a SETUP transaction on a control endpoint. This bit changes its value only for control endpoints. It is cleared by hardware when the SETUP transaction is acknowledged and VTTX interrupt generated.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 12w02 STATRX (rw): Status bits, for reception transfers Device mode These bits contain information about the endpoint status, which are listed in Reception status encoding on page 2492. These bits can be toggled by software to initialize their value. When the application software writes 0, the value remains unchanged, while writing 1 makes the bit value to toggle. Hardware sets the STATRX bits to NAK when a correct transfer has occurred (VTRX = 1) corresponding to a OUT or SETUP (control only) transaction addressed to this endpoint, so the software has the time to elaborate the received data before it acknowledges a new transaction. Double-buffered bulk endpoints implement a special transaction flow control, which control the status based upon buffer availability condition (Refer to endpoints and usage in Device mode). If the endpoint is defined as isochronous, its status can be only 'VALID' or 'DISABLED', so that the hardware cannot change the status of the endpoint after a successful transaction. If the software sets the STATRX bits to 'STALL' or 'NAK' for an isochronous endpoint, the USB peripheral behavior is not defined. These bits are read/write but they can be only toggled by writing 1. Host mode These bits are the host application controls to start, retry, or abort host transactions driven by the channel. These bits also contain information about the device answer to the last IN channel transaction and report the current status of the channel according to the following STATRX table of states: - DISABLE DISABLE value is reported in case of ACK acknowledge is received on a single-buffer channel. When in DISABLE state the channel is unused or not active waiting for application to restart it by writing VALID. Application can reset a VALID channel to DISABLE to abort a transaction. In this case the transaction is immediately removed from the host execution list. If the aborted transaction was already under execution it is regularly terminated on the USB but the relative VTRX interrupt is not generated. - VALID A host channel is actively trying to submit USB transaction to device only when in VALID state.VALID state can be set by software or automatically by hardware on a NAKED channel at the start of a new frame. When set to VALID, an host channel enters the host execution queue and waits permission from the host frame scheduler to submit its configured transaction. VALID value is also reported in case of ACK acknowledge is received on a double-buffered channel. In this case the channel remains active on the alternate buffer while application needs to read the current buffer and toggle DTOGTX. In case software is late in reading and the alternate buffer is not ready, the host channel is automatically suspended transparently to the application. The suspended double buffered channel is re-activated as soon as delay is recovered and DTOGTX is toggled. - NAK NAK value is reported in case of NAK acknowledge received. When in NAK state the channel is suspended and does not try to transmit. NAK state is moved to VALID by hardware at the start of the next frame, or software can change it to immediately retry transmission by writing it to VALID, or can disable it and abort the transaction by writing DISABLE - STALL STALL value is reported in case of STALL acknowledge received. When in STALL state the channel behaves as disabled. Application should not retry transmission but reset the USB and re-enumerate.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 14w01 DTOGRX (rw): Data Toggle, for reception transfers If the endpoint/channel is not isochronous, this bit contains the expected value of the data toggle bit (0 = DATA0, 1 = DATA1) for the next data packet to be received. Hardware toggles this bit, when the ACK handshake is sent following a data packet reception having a matching data PID value; if the endpoint is defined as a control one, hardware clears this bit at the reception of a SETUP PID received from host (in device) or acknowledged by device (in host). If the endpoint/channel is using the double-buffering feature this bit is used to support packet buffer swapping too (Refer to Device mode). If the endpoint/channel is isochronous, this bit is used only to support packet buffer swapping for data transmission since no data toggling is used for this kind of channels/endpoints and only DATA0 packet are transmitted (Refer to Isochronous transfers in Device mode). Hardware toggles this bit just after the end of data packet reception, since no handshake is used for isochronous transfers. This bit can also be toggled by the software to initialize its value (mandatory when the endpoint is not a control one) or to force specific data toggle/packet buffer usage. When the application software writes 0, the value of DTOGRX remains unchanged, while writing 1 makes the bit value toggle. This bit is read/write but it can be only toggled by writing 1.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 15w01 VTRX (rw): USB valid transaction received Device mode This bit is set by the hardware when an OUT/SETUP transaction is successfully completed on this endpoint; the software can only clear this bit. If the CTRM bit in USB_CNTR register is set accordingly, a generic interrupt condition is generated together with the endpoint related interrupt condition, which is always activated. The type of occurred transaction, OUT or SETUP, can be determined from the SETUP bit described below. A transaction ended with a NAK or STALL handshake does not set this bit, since no data is actually transferred, as in the case of protocol errors or data toggle mismatches. This bit is read/write but only 0 can be written, writing 1 has no effect. Host mode This bit is set by the hardware when an IN transaction is successfully completed on this channel. The software can only clear this bit. If the CTRM bit in USB_CNTR register is set a generic interrupt condition is generated together with the channel related flag, which is always activated. - A transaction ended with a NAK sets this bit and NAK answer is reported to application reading the NAK state from the STATRX field of this register. One NAKed transaction keeps pending and is automatically retried by the host at the next frame, or the host can immediately retry by resetting STATRX state to VALID. - A transaction ended by STALL handshake sets this bit and the STALL answer is reported to application reading the STALL state from the STATRX field of this register. Host application should consequently disable the channel and re-enumerate. - A transaction ended with ACK handshake sets this bit If double buffering is disabled, ACK answer is reported by application reading the DISABLE state from the STATRX field of this register. Host application should read received data from USBRAM and re-arm the channel by writing VALID to the STATRX field of this register. If double buffering is enabled, ACK answer is reported by application reading VALID state from the STATRX field of this register. Host application should read received data from USBRAM and toggle the DTOGTX bit of this register. - A transaction ended with error sets this bit. Errors can be seen via the bits ERR_RX (host mode only). This bit is read/write but only 0 can be written, writing 1 has no effect.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 16w07 DEVADDR (rw): Host mode Device address assigned to the endpoint during the enumeration process.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 23w01 NAK (rw): Host mode This bit is set by the hardware when a device responds with a NAK. Software can use this bit to monitor the number of NAKs received from a device.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 24w01 LS_EP (rw): Low speed endpoint host with HUB only Host mode This bit is set by the software to send an LS transaction to the corresponding endpoint.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 25w01 ERR_TX (rw): Received error for an OUT/SETUP transaction Host mode This bit is set by the hardware when an error (for example no answer by the device, CRC error, bit stuffing error, framing format violation, etc.) has occurred during an OUT or SETUP transaction on this channel. The software can only clear this bit. If the ERRM bit in USB_CNTR register is set, a generic interrupt condition is generated together with the channel related flag, which is always activated.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 26w01 ERR_RX (rw): Received error for an IN transaction Host mode This bit is set by the hardware when an error (for example no answer by the device, CRC error, bit stuffing error, framing format violation, etc.) has occurred during an IN transaction on this channel. The software can only clear this bit. If the ERRM bit in USB_CNTR register is set, a generic interrupt condition is generated together with the channel related flag, which is always activated.
0x50016000 C   FIELD 27w02 THREE_ERR_TX (rw): Three errors for an OUT or SETUP transaction Host mode This bit is set by the hardware when 3 consecutive transaction errors occurred on the USB bus for an OUT transaction. THREE_ERR_TX is not generated for isochronous transactions. The software can only clear this bit. Coding of the received error:
0x50016000 C   FIELD 29w02 THREE_ERR_RX (rw): Three errors for an IN transaction Host mode This bit is set by the hardware when 3 consecutive transaction errors occurred on the USB bus for an IN transaction. THREE_ERR_RX is not generated for isochronous transactions. The software can only clear this bit. Coding of the received error:
0x50016004 B  REGISTER CHEP1R: USB endpoint/channel 1 register
